-- FRELUX: backfill article FAQs from each article's own question headings.
--
-- The Learn library has 392 published articles but only 91 carry FAQs. This
-- migration adds the missing questions for the 95 articles whose own section
-- headings are already phrased as questions. Every answer is the article's
-- first sentences under that heading, so nothing here is new copy and nothing
-- asserts a fact the article does not already state.
--
-- Idempotent: rows are inserted only when the same question is not already on
-- the article, and sort_order continues after the existing rows.
--
-- Articles whose headings are statements (272 of them) are deliberately NOT
-- filled here. Turning "Phase 1: Foundation" into a question would produce
-- templated FAQ copy across the library, which is a content decision, not a
-- data fix.

BEGIN;

INSERT INTO learn_article_faqs (article_id, question, answer, sort_order)
SELECT a.id, v.question, v.answer, v.sort_order
FROM (VALUES
  ('alc-exterior-coating-materials', 1, 'Why must the sealer be ALC-specific?', 'The sealer must suit ALC''s absorption, which is high and uneven compared with dense concrete. An ALC sealer penetrates the porous surface and equalises it so the base coat dries at a consistent rate.'),
  ('alc-exterior-coating-materials', 2, 'What makes a base coat elastic?', 'A base coat is elastic when its polymer composition gives it elongation at the specified thickness, and that property is stated on the data sheet. Elongation percentage at a given film thickness is the number that matters.'),
  ('alc-exterior-coating-materials', 3, 'Does the top coat do anything beyond colour?', 'The top coat carries the weather resistance: ultraviolet stability, dirt resistance and water repellency. It is the layer that chalks and fades, and its life sets the recoating cycle.'),
  ('alc-exterior-coating-materials', 4, 'When is mesh or reinforcing strip used?', 'Mesh is used where the substrate has cracked or where a joint has a history of movement beyond the coating''s capacity. It is a repair detail rather than a standard layer. A facade with recurring cracks along a joint line gets a strip over that line before the base coat, which spreads the movement over a wider band of coating.'),
  ('alc-exterior-coating-materials', 5, 'Which products suit a coastal or exposed site?', 'A coastal site needs a system with better resistance to salt, ultraviolet and wind-driven rain, and it needs a shorter inspection interval. Salt attacks the coating surface and accelerates chalking; wind-driven rain finds every detail.'),
  ('alc-exterior-coating-materials', 6, 'How much material does a facade need?', 'Material quantities follow area, coats and the product''s coverage rate, with the joint length as a separate line for the sealant. A facade with many openings has more joint per square metre, so a sealant estimate taken from area alone comes up short.'),
  ('alc-panel-materials-sealants-coatings', 1, 'What are ALC panels made of and how are they graded?', 'An ALC panel is made of cement, lime, silica sand or fly ash, water and an expansion agent, cast with reinforcement and cured in an autoclave. The result is a lightweight cellular concrete with a fine, uniform pore structure.'),
  ('alc-panel-materials-sealants-coatings', 2, 'Which sealant actually keeps water out?', 'The sealant has to be elastic, adhere to ALC and tolerate the movement of a joint between two rigid panels on a deflecting frame. Silicone sealants dominate Japanese ALC facades because they stay elastic for years and adhere well to a prepared ALC surface.'),
  ('alc-panel-materials-sealants-coatings', 3, 'Which coating suits a humid climate?', 'Japan''s humidity and summer heat put a premium on coatings that resist dirt pick-up, mould growth and ultraviolet degradation. A dirt-resistant top coat keeps the facade looking sound for longer, and an anti-mould additive matters on north elevations that stay damp.'),
  ('alc-panel-materials-sealants-coatings', 4, 'Which panel thickness suits which wall?', 'Panel thickness is chosen against a fire requirement, an insulation requirement and a span, and the thickest option is not automatically the right one. A 100 mm panel is the common exterior infill, and it satisfies many low and mid-rise walls on its own.'),
  ('alc-panel-materials-sealants-coatings', 5, 'Does the coating colour affect durability?', 'Colour affects durability more than most buyers expect, and light colours always last better on ALC. A dark facade absorbs solar heat and expands further than a light one, which puts more movement into the joints and the coating.'),
  ('alc-panel-materials-sealants-coatings', 6, 'What does a sealant cost per metre?', 'Sealant is priced by linear metre, and the price rises steeply with access height rather than with the product. The material is a small part of the cost per metre. The preparation, the backer rod, the priming and the tooling are the labour, and the access is the multiplier.'),
  ('alc-panel-wall-rc-frame-overview', 1, 'What is an ALC panel wall and why is it used?', 'An ALC panel wall is a cladding of autoclaved lightweight concrete panels, typically 100 mm thick, fixed vertically or horizontally to a concrete or steel frame and finished with a joint sealant and a coating.'),
  ('alc-panel-wall-rc-frame-overview', 2, 'How thick are ALC panels and what do they weigh?', 'Standard ALC panels are commonly 100 mm thick, with thicker panels used where the wall carries a fire or insulation requirement. Density is roughly a quarter that of normal concrete, so the panels are light enough to handle by hand with two people.'),
  ('alc-panel-wall-rc-frame-overview', 3, 'Where does the water get in?', 'Water gets into an ALC wall at the joints, the openings and any crack, not through the panel face. The panels themselves are coated, and a sound coating sheds water. The vulnerable line is the joint between panels, which is filled with a sealant that must accommodate movement between two rigid panels on a frame that deflects.'),
  ('alc-panel-wall-rc-frame-overview', 4, 'Does ALC need insulation?', 'ALC provides some insulation itself, which is why the panels are sometimes used without added insulation on lower buildings. Its thermal performance is moderate rather than high, so colder regions and higher-performance buildings add insulation either outside or within the frame.'),
  ('alc-panel-wall-rc-frame-overview', 5, 'How long does an ALC facade last?', 'The panels last decades if they stay dry and their coating is maintained. The sealant and the coating are the parts that wear out, on cycles measured in years rather than decades. A maintained facade, recoated on the manufacturer''s schedule with the joints inspected each time, stays serviceable for the life of the building.'),
  ('alc-panel-wall-rc-frame-overview', 6, 'Can you cut or alter an ALC panel wall?', 'You can cut ALC, and that is exactly why alterations are risky. Cutting a panel for a new opening is straightforward with the right tool, but the cut removes the factory edge, exposes the cellular interior and interrupts the coating and the reinforcement.'),
  ('au-double-brick-installation', 1, 'What is checked before the first course?', 'Footing, damp-proof course, setout and levels. The footing must be cured and level, the damp-proof course continuous and correctly positioned, and the setout checked against the building dimensions and the diagonal.'),
  ('au-double-brick-installation', 2, 'How is the wall laid to keep it plumb and level?', 'With a line and a level at every course, corner profiles or story poles to set the gauge, and a check of the wall face for plumb at intervals rather than at the end. Bricks are laid to a consistent bed and the joints are tooled as the work proceeds, because tooling later disturbs the bond.'),
  ('au-double-brick-installation', 3, 'How are ties installed correctly?', 'Embedded into both leaves, at the specified spacing, with a slight fall toward the outer leaf. The tie goes into the mortar bed, not into a hole drilled afterwards, and it is positioned so it is not sitting in the cavity void where it cannot bond.'),
  ('au-double-brick-installation', 4, 'How is the cavity kept clean?', 'By using a cavity board or a batten pulled up as the work proceeds, by striking mortar off the ties and the inner face, and by cleaning out at the end of each lift. Mortar that falls into the cavity sets on the flashing and blocks the drainage path.'),
  ('au-double-brick-installation', 5, 'When are lintels and openings formed?', 'As the wall reaches the opening height. The lintel is bedded with the specified bearing at each end, the brickwork above is built on it, and the flashing at the head of the opening is installed so that water is turned out over the frame.'),
  ('au-double-brick-installation', 6, 'How are articulation joints built in?', 'At the designed positions, as a genuine break in the masonry with a compressible filler and a sealant. The joint is kept free of mortar and the sealant is applied to the specified depth with a backing rod so it can move.'),
  ('au-double-brick-maintenance', 1, 'What is the annual check on a brick wall?', 'A walk around each elevation looking at five things: the articulation joints, the weep holes, the base of the wall, the mortar joints, and any staining. Look for sealant that has pulled away or gone hard, weep holes that have been painted over or blocked by soil or paving, damp staining at the base, mortar that has eroded, and white salt deposits.'),
  ('au-double-brick-maintenance', 2, 'How are weep holes kept open?', 'By clearing them of mortar, paint, soil and vegetation, and by making sure the ground or paving outside is below the level of the flashing so water can discharge. The most common cause of blocked weep holes is not mortar but landscaping: garden beds built up against the wall, or a paved path laid over the outlet.'),
  ('au-double-brick-maintenance', 3, 'When should mortar be repointed?', 'When the joints have eroded to the point that water can sit in them, or when the mortar has become soft and sandy. Repointing is a skilled job: the old mortar is raked out to a depth of about twice the joint width, the joint is cleaned, and new mortar of a compatible mix is packed in and tooled.'),
  ('au-double-brick-maintenance', 4, 'How is efflorescence dealt with?', 'By brushing off the dry salt, then finding the water. Washing with water can drive the salt back into the masonry, and sealing the surface traps moisture and makes the problem worse.'),
  ('au-double-brick-maintenance', 5, 'How is an articulation joint resealed?', 'By removing the old sealant and the backing rod, cleaning the joint faces, priming where the sealant requires it, installing a new backing rod to the correct diameter and depth, and applying a sealant with adequate movement capability.'),
  ('au-double-brick-maintenance', 6, 'What does rising damp look like and what causes it?', 'A horizontal band of damp and salt damage at the base of the wall, usually with blistering plaster inside and eroded mortar outside. The cause is a bridged or failed damp-proof course, or earth built up above it.'),
  ('au-double-brick-materials', 1, 'How is brick exposure graded and why does it matter?', 'Bricks are graded for the conditions they can tolerate, with exposure grades defined for protected, general and severe marine or industrial environments. A brick rated for protected conditions will not perform on a coastal facade exposed to salt spray and driving rain, and the failure mode is spalling and salt attack rather than simple weathering.'),
  ('au-double-brick-materials', 2, 'What mortar should be used?', 'A mortar that is weaker than the bricks and compatible with them. The common mix for general masonry is a cement, lime and sand mortar in proportions around one part cement to one part lime to six parts sand, adjusted for the exposure and the unit being laid.'),
  ('au-double-brick-materials', 3, 'What do wall ties do and which type should be used?', 'Ties transfer wind load between the leaves and hold the outer leaf to the inner one. They come in a range of materials: galvanised steel for protected situations, heavily coated or stainless steel for coastal and severe exposures, and proprietary types for specific cavity widths and insulation.'),
  ('au-double-brick-materials', 4, 'What keeps water out at openings and at the base?', 'Flashings and the damp-proof course. A flashing at the base of the cavity collects water that has run down the inner face of the outer leaf and discharges it through weep holes. Flashings at window heads, sills and roof junctions do the same job locally, turning water outward and protecting the masonry below.'),
  ('au-double-brick-materials', 5, 'How are weep holes formed and kept open?', 'With formers that are removed after laying, or with proprietary vents that stay in place. They are placed at defined intervals along the base of the cavity and above flashings, and their only job is to let water out and air in.'),
  ('au-double-brick-materials', 6, 'What is used for reinforcement and lintels?', 'Steel reinforcement in the form of joint reinforcement or rod in the mortar bed, placed where the design calls for it, usually to control cracking over long panels or at stress concentrations.'),
  ('au-double-brick-overview', 1, 'What is the difference between double brick and brick veneer?', 'In a double brick wall both leaves are structural masonry, with the inner leaf carrying the floor and roof loads and the outer leaf providing the weather skin, tied to the inner leaf and sharing the stability of the assembly.'),
  ('au-double-brick-overview', 2, 'Why does the cavity matter so much?', 'Because it is the drainage plane. Wind-driven rain will wet the outer face, and some water will penetrate the brickwork itself, particularly at mortar joints, around openings and where the brick is saturated.'),
  ('au-double-brick-overview', 3, 'How do the two leaves work together structurally?', 'Through the ties, and through the way the leaves are loaded. The inner leaf carries vertical load. The outer leaf is supported on its own footing or on the same footing, and is held to the inner leaf by wall ties at a specified spacing.'),
  ('au-double-brick-overview', 4, 'Where does movement get accommodated?', 'In articulation joints. Masonry expands and contracts, and a long wall with no joints will crack where it chooses. Articulation joints are placed at defined intervals and at changes of geometry or height, and they are sealed with a flexible sealant so the joint can move without letting water in.'),
  ('au-double-brick-overview', 5, 'What does the inside face look like?', 'Older double brick houses often have the inner leaf plastered directly, with a hard or a soft plaster finish. Newer construction more often uses a plasterboard lining on battens or furring channels fixed to the inner leaf, which provides a cavity for services, improves thermal performance and gives a flatter wall.'),
  ('au-double-brick-overview', 6, 'How does the wall handle moisture from inside?', 'By diffusion and ventilation. A double brick wall is a vapour-open assembly: moisture from inside the house moves through the plaster and the masonry and evaporates from the outer face.'),
  ('au-double-brick-quality', 1, 'What can only be inspected during construction?', 'Four things. The base flashing and its turn-up. The tie spacing and their fall to the outer leaf. The cleanliness of the cavity. And the damp-proof course continuity at the base. Each is a permanent, hidden decision.'),
  ('au-double-brick-quality', 2, 'How is the cavity checked for obstructions?', 'At the base, with a light, looking for mortar on the flashing. At openings, where the cavity is exposed, by reaching in and feeling for a shelf of mortar. And by counting weep holes and confirming they are open rather than formed and filled.'),
  ('au-double-brick-quality', 3, 'What is an acceptable joint finish?', 'One that is consistent across the wall, tooled to the specified profile, and free of voids and excessive smearing. The profile affects weather performance: a recessed or weathered joint sheds water better than a flush joint that collects it.'),
  ('au-double-brick-quality', 4, 'How is plumb and level checked?', 'With a level or a plumb bob at intervals along the wall, and a straight edge across the face to check for hollows and bows. Deviations within the tolerance are acceptable; a wall that leans because the setout was wrong is not.'),
  ('au-double-brick-quality', 5, 'How is water penetration tested or assessed?', 'Not by hosing a new wall, which proves little and can cause staining. It is assessed by design review and by detail inspection: the flashings, the weep holes, the articulation joints and the roof connection.'),
  ('au-double-brick-quality', 6, 'What does efflorescence indicate?', 'That salts have moved through the masonry in water and crystallised on the surface as the water evaporated. It indicates moisture movement rather than structural failure, and it is common on new walls as the mortar cures.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-installation', 1, 'What preparation comes before the first coat?', 'The wall cleaned, the surface keyed, and any absorbent or uneven background dealt with. Dust and loose mortar must come off, because render adheres to a clean substrate only. Highly absorbent blockwork needs a pre-wet or a bonding coat so it does not pull the water out of the mix before the cement has set.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-installation', 2, 'How is the base coat applied?', 'Trowelled on and then scratched or keyed while green, to give the finish coat something to grip. Thickness is set by the beads and the plane of the wall, typically 8 to 12 millimetres.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-installation', 3, 'When does the finish coat go on?', 'While the base coat is still green, typically the same day or the next, depending on weather. That window is the whole reason renderers work in pairs and in sequence along an elevation.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-installation', 4, 'How is the surface finished?', 'Sponged, floated, or left with a texture applied over it, depending on the specified look. Sponging closes the surface and gives an even tooth for paint. Floating leaves a slightly coarser plane that shows texture under raking sun.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-installation', 5, 'How is the render cured?', 'Damp for three to seven days, protected from sun and wind, without being flooded. Hessian kept wet, plastic sheeting on a shaded elevation, or a curing membrane are the practical options.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-installation', 6, 'When can paint go on?', 'After the render has cured and dried, usually a minimum of seven days, and ideally four weeks for a full carbonate set. The painter''s test is moisture, not the calendar: a moisture meter reading, or a plastic sheet taped over the wall for a day, tells whether the wall is still giving off water.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-maintenance', 1, 'What does annual maintenance involve?', 'A wash down, a walk-around inspection at eye level and above openings, and a check of every sealant joint. Washing removes the chalk, salt and pollution film that keeps a coating from performing.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-maintenance', 2, 'How often should the facade be recoated?', 'Every seven to twelve years, and sooner on coastal or dark-coloured walls. The signal is the film, not the calendar: chalk that comes off on a hand, colour that has faded unevenly, or a surface that holds dirt after washing.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-maintenance', 3, 'How are cracks repaired?', 'Hairline shrinkage cracks are filled with a flexible filler and left; live cracks are converted into movement joints with sealant. The distinction matters because filling a live crack with rigid material guarantees it reopens.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-maintenance', 4, 'How is sealant maintained?', 'Inspected annually, cut out and replaced every eight to twelve years, and always replaced where it has lost adhesion. Sealant is the weakest material in the system and the most important for water control.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-maintenance', 5, 'What is different on the coast?', 'Salt accelerates everything: chalk formation, colour fade, metal corrosion at beads and fixings, and sealant degradation. Washing becomes twice-yearly in exposed positions, recoating moves to the shorter end of the interval, and stainless fixings and beads are specified at the start rather than discovered as rust streaks later.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-maintenance', 6, 'How is efflorescence treated?', 'Brush it off dry, find the water source, and only then consider a water repellent. Efflorescence is salt carried to the surface by water evaporating from the wall. Treating the symptom with a sealer traps the salt and pushes the damage inwards.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-materials', 1, 'What is the standard render mix?', 'Four parts sand to one part cement by volume, with lime or a polymer added for workability and adhesion. Sharp, well-graded sand matters more than most owners realise. A sand with too much clay makes a weak render that dusts; a sand that is too coarse makes it harsh and hard to float.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-materials', 2, 'Does adding more cement make it stronger?', 'No, it makes it more brittle. A rich mix shrinks harder and cracks sooner. The instinct to add cement for durability is the most common mixing error in rendered work. The strength that matters is tensile and adhesive, and both come from the sand grading, the water retention and the curing, not from extra cement.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-materials', 3, 'What is mesh for?', 'Reinforcing the render at stress concentrations, not reinforcing the wall. Mesh is laid in the base coat at the corners of openings, at junctions between different substrates, and across the full surface where the background is weak or where the render is thin.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-materials', 4, 'What about beads and trims?', 'Beads give a straight arris at openings and a stop where the render ends; trims form movement joints and drip details. They are fixed before the base coat and bedded in it, so the render holds them rather than the other way round.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-materials', 5, 'Which sealant goes into movement joints?', 'A low-modulus, exterior-grade polyurethane or hybrid sealant with a backing rod to set the depth. The joint must be able to move plus or minus 25 per cent without the sealant tearing or the render edges spalling.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-materials', 6, 'What is the difference between render and texture coating?', 'Texture coating is a coloured acrylic applied over the render in one or more coats, giving colour and texture together. Rendered and painted walls use two systems: cement render plus paint, or render plus a texture coating that acts as both finish and colour.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-overview', 1, 'What is render for?', 'Levelling the wall, protecting the masonry, and providing a uniform surface for the coating. On brick veneer, the render covers the brick and the mortar joints, which are the paths water takes into the wall.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-overview', 2, 'What are the coats?', 'A base coat keyed for adhesion, and a finish coat at the texture and colour required. On strong masonry the base coat can be a single coat; on absorbent or uneven backgrounds, a spatter or dubbing-out coat comes first to bring the wall to a consistent plane.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-overview', 3, 'Why does curing matter more than mixing?', 'Because cement needs water to gain strength, and render dries too fast in Australian sun. Curing means keeping the surface damp for several days after application. Uncovered render on a north-facing wall can lose its water within hours, which leaves a weak, dusty, friable surface that will not hold paint.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-overview', 4, 'What causes cracking?', 'Movement, either of the substrate or within the render, and shrinkage as the cement cures. Render cracks at stress concentrations: corners of openings, junctions between materials, long unbroken panels, and where a wall changes direction.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-overview', 5, 'What are movement joints?', 'Deliberate discontinuities that let the render move without cracking, sealed with an elastic joint sealant. They belong at intervals along long walls, at changes of substrate, and at the junctions between a rendered wall and a different material.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-overview', 6, 'How is the render finished?', 'By the surface treatment chosen: sponge, float, scraped, or a texture coating applied over the render. A sponge finish closes the surface and gives a fine even tooth. A float finish leaves a slightly coarser texture.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-quality', 1, 'How is render checked for hollowness?', 'By tapping with a hammer handle or a light tapping tool across the elevation, at close intervals, listening for a change in sound. A hollow note means the render has separated from the substrate.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-quality', 2, 'What crack types matter?', 'Live cracks that move, shrinkage cracks that do not, and structural cracks that indicate a problem behind the render. Shrinkage map-cracking is fine and cosmetic. A single crack running from the corner of an opening, or along a floor line, is live movement and needs a movement joint or a repaired detail.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-quality', 3, 'How is the plane checked?', 'With a straightedge and a low-angle light along the wall, in both directions. Render should be flat to within a few millimetres over a metre and consistent across the elevation. Belly and hollow read as a wave when light rakes across the wall, most obviously on a smooth sponged finish.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-quality', 4, 'How is cure verified before coating?', 'By rubbing the surface with a dry hand for dusting, and by a moisture reading or a plastic sheet test for residual water. A dusty surface means the cement never hydrated properly; the coating will bond to the dust rather than the render.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-quality', 5, 'How is film thickness judged?', 'By coverage arithmetic against material used, and by visual inspection for thin areas at edges and in texture hollows. Manufacturers state a spreading rate. Dividing the area by the litres used gives an average film, and a figure well below the stated rate means the coats are thin somewhere even if they look even.'),
  ('au-exterior-render-paint-quality', 6, 'What do lap marks indicate?', 'That a coat was applied in sun or wind, or that a wet edge was lost between sections. Lap marks are visible as bands of slightly different sheen, and they are permanent once the film has cured.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-installation', 1, 'How are the sheets laid out before fixing?', 'Start from the ceiling and work down, and arrange the joints on the framing rather than wherever a sheet happens to end. Sheets are laid horizontally on walls where height allows, so the long joint runs around the room at a comfortable height.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-installation', 2, 'How are sheets fixed to a timber frame?', 'Screws at 200 to 300 millimetre centres on studs, and 200 millimetres around the perimeter, with the screw heads set just below the paper without breaking it. A screw driven too deep tears the paper and destroys the holding power; a screw left proud will show through the finished surface.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-installation', 3, 'How does a steel frame change the fixing?', 'Self-drilling screws replace the timber screws and the centres stay similar, but the screw must not spin in the thin steel. Over-driving is the common failure, and it is easy to do because the steel gives no resistance once the thread has cut through.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-installation', 4, 'How is the first jointing pass done?', 'Embed the tape in a bedding compound across every joint and internal corner, and spot every screw head in the same operation. The tape is pressed into wet compound, then a thin layer is drawn over it.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-installation', 5, 'Why are three passes needed?', 'Because each pass adds width, not thickness, and the joint has to fade out over 200 to 300 millimetres to disappear. The second pass covers the tape with a wider band. The third feathers the edges to nothing.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-installation', 6, 'How is sanding done without wrecking the wall?', 'With a pole sander and 120 to 150 grit, working along the joint rather than across it, under a light held at a low angle. Raking light shows the ridges that a frontal light hides. The object is to flatten the feather, not to remove the compound, and sanding through to the paper leaves a scuffed surface that the sealer will not hide.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-maintenance', 1, 'How is a small hole repaired?', 'Clean the edges, bridge the hole with a patch or with tape, fill in two passes, sand, seal and repaint that wall. Holes under about fifty millimetres can be filled directly if the paper around them is intact.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-maintenance', 2, 'How is a hole too big for a patch repaired?', 'By cutting a square of board to fit and screwing it to added timber backing inside the cavity. The cut-out is squared with a knife, the backing timber is fixed through the surrounding board, and the new piece is screwed in flush.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-maintenance', 3, 'How are screw pops repaired?', 'Drive a new screw 50 millimetres away from the popped one, set it correctly, then remove or recess the old screw and fill the dimple. Simply filling over a pop fails, because the cause is a screw that has lost its grip.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-maintenance', 4, 'How are joint cracks treated?', 'Open the crack slightly, fill with a setting compound, embed tape if the crack is more than a hairline, then feather and sand. Filling a crack without tape is a cosmetic repair that reopens.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-maintenance', 5, 'How is the repair blended into the existing paint?', 'By repainting to a natural break, such as a corner or the whole wall, rather than spotting in the middle. Touch-up paint never matches after a few months, because the wall has aged and the paint in the tin has not.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-maintenance', 6, 'How often should an interior be repainted?', 'Every seven to ten years in a well-ventilated home, and more often in kitchens, bathrooms and hallways. The signal is not the calendar but the surface: a wall that no longer wipes clean, or that shows a chalky film when rubbed, has lost its film and needs recoating.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-materials', 1, 'What plasterboard thickness should be used?', 'Ten millimetres on ceilings and walls in ordinary residential work, thirteen millimetres where the wall is fire-rated, impact-loaded or required to hold a higher acoustic rating. Thicker board is stiffer, which reduces deflection between studs and gives a flatter wall, but it costs more and weighs more.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-materials', 2, 'What is the difference between the board types?', 'Standard board for dry areas, moisture-resistant board for bathrooms, laundries and kitchens, fire-rated board for walls on a fire-separation line, and acoustic board where a sound rating is specified.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-materials', 3, 'Which compounds are used in Australia?', 'Three functional types: a base or bedding compound for embedding tape, a topping compound for the final feather, and a all-purpose compound that does both. Base compounds are stronger and shrink less; toppings sand more easily.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-materials', 4, 'What tape goes into the joint?', 'Paper tape is the default in Australian residential work, bedded in compound and covered while wet. Paper tape is strong in tension and resists cracking along the joint better than mesh.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-materials', 5, 'What about cornice and trims?', 'Cornice covers the junction of wall and ceiling and hides the movement that occurs there. It is fixed with adhesive and screws, or with a cornice cement where the profile allows. Corner beads protect external corners and give a straight arris.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-materials', 6, 'Which sealer and paint suit a new lining?', 'A penetrating or alkali-resistant sealer on the new board, then two coats of low sheen acrylic for most domestic rooms. Sealer does two jobs: it equalises absorption so the topcoat does not dry patchy, and it binds the surface dust that sanding leaves behind.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-overview', 1, 'What does "plasterboard to paint" actually involve?', 'It is a three-stage process: mechanical fixing, joint treatment, and coating. Mechanical fixing means the sheets are screwed to the studs at set centres with the joints arranged on framing.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-overview', 2, 'Why does the joint treatment decide the result?', 'Because a taped joint is a shallow depression that has to be filled in a way that does not show under raking light. The tape bridges the gap; the compounds feather the fill out over 200 to 300 millimetres each side so the transition disappears.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-overview', 3, 'What level of finish should an Australian home have?', 'Level 4 is the normal domestic standard, and it is what a client is usually buying when nothing is specified. Level 4 means all joints and corners are taped and coated with three passes of compound, screw heads are spotted, and the finish is suitable for flat paint under ordinary lighting.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-overview', 4, 'Where does the moisture go?', 'Into the frame cavity, if the wall is detailed properly, and into the lining if it is not. Plasterboard is dimensionally stable until it is wet, and then it swells, sags and stains.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-overview', 5, 'How long does the sequence take?', 'For a typical house, jointing and sanding runs three to four working days after the sheets are fixed, then the painter takes two to three days including drying. Drying dominates. Compound needs to dry between passes, and paint needs its recoat window.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-overview', 6, 'What does it cost?', 'As a rough Australian guide, stopping and finishing a plasterboard wall runs about 12 to 22 dollars per square metre of wall area, depending on level of finish and ceiling height. Painting a prepared interior wall runs about 8 to 15 dollars per square metre for two coats over a sealer, with the lower end for large uncluttered areas and the higher end for rooms with lots of cutting in.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-quality', 1, 'When should the stopping be inspected?', 'After sanding and before the sealer, with the room lit by a work light held close to the wall at a low angle. That combination shows every ridge, dimple and proud screw head. The same wall under a central ceiling light looks perfect, which is why inspections done in daylight from the doorway pass walls that fail the moment a lamp is placed against them.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-quality', 2, 'What counts as a ridge?', 'A visible change in plane at a joint, detectable by eye under raking light, and by hand as a step rather than a wave. A wave is normal in a level 4 finish. A step is not: it means the joint was built too thick or feathered too abruptly, and it will show under the finish coat.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-quality', 3, 'How are screw heads judged?', 'Every head should be a shallow dimple filled flush, with the paper intact around it. A head set too deep has broken the paper and lost holding power; it will pop. A head left proud shows as a bump.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-quality', 4, 'What about cornice lines and corners?', 'The cornice line should be straight along the whole wall, and external corners should be sharp without being fragile. Checked with a string line at the cornice and by eye along the arris.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-quality', 5, 'How is paint coverage checked?', 'By counting coats and inspecting for thin areas at edges, corners and behind fittings, not by looking at the wall from the middle of the room. Thin coats show as a slight difference in sheen, especially on a low sheen finish, and are most common where a roller ran dry near a cut-in line.'),
  ('au-interior-plasterboard-paint-quality', 6, 'What defects justify rejecting a wall?', 'Ridges under raking light, visible screw pops, cracked joints, paint thin enough to show the substrate, drips and sags in the finish, and a wavy cornice line. A level 4 finish tolerates a slight wave and does not tolerate a step.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-installation', 1, 'What happens before the first stud goes in?', 'Setout. The wall position is marked on the slab or the floor deck, checked against the drawings, and squared off a known reference. Errors at setout propagate all the way to the skirting and the door reveals, and they are almost impossible to correct later without visible consequences.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-installation', 2, 'How is the frame erected and braced?', 'Tracks are fixed first, then studs are cut to length and located at the specified centres, then the top track goes on and the assembly is squared. Noggins and trimmers go in for board edges and openings, and the bracing is installed to the engineer''s pattern.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-installation', 3, 'How are services coordinated with the frame?', 'Before lining. Electrical cable, data, plumbing and any ducting must be run in the cavity, and the holes for them must comply with the framing limits: a hole in the web of a stud, not a notch out of a flange, and no holes clustered in the same place.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-installation', 4, 'What is the right order for sheeting a wall?', 'Ceilings first, then walls, then the joints, then the cornice. Sheets are lifted, checked for damage, and cut face-up with a sharp knife and a straight edge. The sheet is offered to the wall with the tapered edge at the joints and the long edge across the studs.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-installation', 5, 'How is the jointing built up?', 'In coats, each wider than the last. The first coat fills the recess and beds the tape. The second covers the tape and starts to spread the joint out. The third, and sometimes a fourth, brings the joint flush and feathers the edges so the transition is imperceptible.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-installation', 6, 'How is the wall checked before painting?', 'With a light. A raking light across the wall shows every ridge, hollow and missed screw. The lining contractor should do this check themselves, because the painter will not fix it and the owner will see it every evening when the sun comes in at the right angle.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-maintenance', 1, 'How is a hole in plasterboard repaired so it disappears?', 'By rebuilding the surface in the same way it was built. Small holes are filled with a setting compound, taped if the hole is wider than the taper can bridge, and then coated out in two or three passes with sanding between.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-maintenance', 2, 'Why do screws pop and what should be done about them?', 'Because the board moved and the screw did not, or because the screw was driven too deep and lost its grip in the first place. A pop is a screw head pushing through the paper, usually with a small circular crack around it.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-maintenance', 3, 'What causes cracks at door and window corners?', 'Stress concentration. The corner of an opening is where the wall changes shape, and a framed opening concentrates movement. Cracks there are common, and most are cosmetic. A hairline crack is filled, taped and refinished.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-maintenance', 4, 'How does corrosion become an issue in a steel-framed wall?', 'Through water. A steel stud in a dry internal cavity does not corrode. A stud that has stood in water, or a bottom track that has held water, will corrode from the inside, and the visible signs are staining at the skirting line, a musty smell, or paint blistering low on the wall.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-maintenance', 5, 'How are wet area walls maintained?', 'By keeping the waterproofing intact. The maintenance tasks are re-sealing movement joints and penetrations with the specified sealant, repairing any cracked tile or grout promptly, and checking that the shower wastes and tap penetrations are sound.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-maintenance', 6, 'What is the repainting cycle and what does it cost?', 'Internal walls in Australia are typically repainted every seven to ten years, sooner in high-traffic rental properties and later in a well-kept owner-occupied home. The cost is driven by preparation and by the number of coats rather than by paint quality alone.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-materials', 1, 'Which steel members go into a residential wall frame?', 'Studs and tracks, plus the bracing and the connectors that hold them together. The stud is the vertical member. The track is the horizontal channel at top and bottom that the studs sit into.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-materials', 2, 'What holds the frame together and keeps it square?', 'Bracing, and it is the component most often compromised by later trades. Strap bracing is a galvanised steel strap fixed diagonally across studs in a defined pattern, tensioned and nailed or screwed at each end.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-materials', 3, 'Which plasterboard thickness and type should be used?', 'Ten millimetre board for ordinary walls and ceilings, thirteen millimetre where fire resistance, impact resistance or a better acoustic result is specified, and moisture-resistant board in bathrooms, laundries and behind tiles.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-materials', 4, 'What fasteners and adhesives are used, and why both?', 'Screws alone will hold a board. Screws plus a stud adhesive will hold it better and reduce the number of fasteners needed, because the adhesive spreads the load and the screws hold the board until the adhesive sets.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-materials', 5, 'What jointing materials are needed?', 'Compound, tape, and the beads. Jointing compound comes as a setting type, which hardens chemically and is used for the first coats and for filling, and as a drying type, which is used for finishing coats because it sands more easily.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-materials', 6, 'What goes behind and inside the wall?', 'Insulation, vapour control, and services. Insulation batts sit between studs for thermal and acoustic performance, and they must fill the cavity without being compressed. A vapour control layer or a sealed lining strategy keeps moist air out of the cavity, and it is only effective if it is continuous, including behind the bottom track and around penetrations.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-overview', 1, 'What does a light steel frame actually consist of?', 'A wall frame is a set of vertical studs sitting inside a top track and a bottom track. The studs are cold-formed steel sections, commonly C-shaped, with the tracks being simpler U-sections that hold the studs at top and bottom.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-overview', 2, 'Why has steel replaced timber in so much Australian housing?', 'Three reasons, in order of importance to the people paying. First, termites. A steel frame cannot be eaten, so the frame drops out of the termite management problem entirely, which simplifies the compliance story in high-risk areas.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-overview', 3, 'Does a steel frame need different plasterboard?', 'No, the board is the same family of gypsum plasterboard used over timber. What changes is the fixing detail. Steel studs are thin, so screws must be self-drilling and must be driven to dimple the paper without tearing it.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-overview', 4, 'What does the jointing level mean in practice?', 'The level of finish defines how flat and how free of visible joint evidence the wall must be, and it is agreed before the jointing starts. A level suitable for a ceiling painted with a flat finish under diffuse light is not the same as a level suitable for a wall where a critical light will rake across it and show every ridge.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-overview', 5, 'Where does a steel-framed wall fail first?', 'Four places, and all four are predictable. Screw pops appear where screws were driven too deep or too shallow, or where the frame has moved. Cracks appear at the corners of openings and at board joints where the frame was not straight or the sheet was forced into place.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-overview', 6, 'What should an owner check before the lining goes on?', 'Three things, all cheap to check and expensive to fix later. The frame should be straight and plumb, with studs at the specified centres and noggins where the specification calls for them.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-quality', 1, 'What tolerance is reasonable for a wall frame?', 'The frame should be plumb, straight and square within the tolerances the framing standard sets, and those tolerances are tighter than most owners expect and looser than most plasterers would like.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-quality', 2, 'How are fixings checked?', 'By looking at the screw heads and by counting. Screws should be at the specified centres along each stud, closer at sheet edges, and set so the head dimples the paper without cutting it.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-quality', 3, 'How is bracing verified?', 'From the engineer''s drawing, against what is actually in the wall, before lining. Each braced panel is identified, the strap or sheet is confirmed, and the end fixings are checked for the specified fasteners and tension.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-quality', 4, 'What does each level of finish actually require?', 'A basic level is suitable where the wall will be painted with a flat finish and viewed in ordinary light. A mid level, which is where most residential work sits, requires joints filled, taped and coated so that no joint or fastener is visible under normal lighting.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-quality', 5, 'What defects should be recorded rather than repaired?', 'All of them. A defect record with photographs, location and description is the basis for a repair agreement, and it distinguishes workmanship defects from movement that happens after handover.'),
  ('au-light-steel-frame-quality', 6, 'How are wet areas inspected differently?', 'Wet areas are inspected for waterproofing continuity rather than finish. The board type must match the location, the waterproofing must be continuous and turned up the wall, penetrations must be sealed with the specified system, and the fall to the waste must be verified before tiling.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-installation', 1, 'Como começar o assentamento?', 'Pela marcação e pelo prumo. A primeira fiada define a parede inteira: se ela sai fora de esquadro, tudo o que vem depois herda o erro, e o revestimento não corrige desvio de prumo relevante.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-installation', 2, 'Como tratar encontros, amarrações e vergas?', 'Com amarração entre paredes nos encontros e vergas sobre vãos. A parede sem amarração se comporta como painel solto, e o primeiro movimento térmico abre a fissura na junção. Verga mal dimensionada produz fissura em diagonal nos cantos do vão.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-installation', 3, 'Quando aplicar o chapisco?', 'Depois da cura inicial da alvenaria e com a superfície limpa. Resíduo de argamassa, poeira e desmoldante impedem a aderência, e o chapisco aplicado sobre base suja descola em placas.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-installation', 4, 'Como executar o emboço?', 'Em faixas, com taliscas para garantir espessura e prumo, e com desempenadeira para regularizar. A espessura deve ser compatível com o que o projeto previu: emboço muito espesso em uma única camada retrai e fissura.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-installation', 5, 'Quando a parede está pronta para a massa?', 'Quando o reboco está curado, seco e sem partes soltas. O teste prático é simples: molhar uma pequena área e observar a absorção; bater de leve e ouvir se há som oco; riscar com a unha e ver se solta pó.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-installation', 6, 'Como aplicar a massa corrida?', 'Em duas ou três demãos finas, com secagem entre elas, e lixamento leve entre demãos. Massa aplicada em camada única e espessa trinca, porque a retração ocorre de forma desigual na espessura.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-maintenance-repair', 1, 'Como corrigir um furo?', 'Com massa corrida em duas demãos, a primeira ligeiramente afundada, a segunda rasando, e lixamento com taco. Massa em camada única e grossa retrai e deixa cova, e a cova aparece justamente sob a luz rasante.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-maintenance-repair', 2, 'Como tratar trincas?', 'Classificando antes de corrigir. Trinca capilar de retração, fina e estabilizada, aceita massa ou massa com tela. Trinca ativa, que abre e fecha com o tempo, exige diagnóstico antes de qualquer reparo.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-maintenance-repair', 3, 'O que fazer com mancha de umidade?', 'Encontrar a origem: vazamento hidráulico, infiltração de fachada, umidade do solo, condensação. Mancha coberta com tinta volta na primeira chuva, e volta mais escura. Depois de tratada a causa e seca a parede, o reparo se faz com fundo isolante e tinta.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-maintenance-repair', 4, 'Como repintar uma parede em bom estado?', 'Limpando, lixando levemente para criar aderência e aplicando fundo preparador se a tinta antiga for brilhante. Duas demãos de tinta nova, com intervalo respeitado. Não é necessário remover a pintura antiga quando ela está firme.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-maintenance-repair', 5, 'Quando é preciso remover a pintura antiga?', 'Quando o filme está soltando, empolando, pulverulento ou quando é incompatível com a tinta nova. Nesses casos, a remoção pode ser mecânica, com lixamento, ou química, com removedor, e o ambiente precisa de ventilação.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-maintenance-repair', 6, 'Como escolher a cor sem errar?', 'Testando na própria parede, em área de pelo menos meio metro quadrado, e observando o resultado em três momentos do dia. Cor de catálogo parece sempre mais clara que na parede, e a incidência de luz muda completamente a leitura.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-materials', 1, 'Como especificar o bloco cerâmico?', 'Pela resistência e pela geometria, não pela aparência. Blocos de vedação têm função não estrutural e não podem receber carga; blocos estruturais são dimensionados para isso. Trocar um pelo outro sem projeto é uma decisão que compromete a segurança da alvenaria.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-materials', 2, 'Qual argamassa usar no assentamento?', 'Uma argamassa com resistência compatível com o bloco, nem mais forte nem mais fraca. Argamassa muito resistente concentra esforços no bloco, que trinca; muito fraca não transfere carga e a parede perde monolitismo.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-materials', 3, 'Por que o chapisco não pode ser dispensado?', 'Porque é ele que garante a aderência entre o bloco e a argamassa do emboço. Sobre a superfície lisa e pouco porosa do bloco de concreto, ou sobre bloco cerâmico com resíduo de desmoldante, o emboço aplicado direto descola em poucos meses.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-materials', 4, 'Que massa corrida escolher?', 'A massa corrida PVA para ambientes secos e internos, e a acrílica para áreas sujeitas a umidade ou maior exigência de resistência. A diferença está na liga: a PVA é mais fácil de lixar e menos resistente à água; a acrílica é mais firme e suporta lavagem.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-materials', 5, 'Qual a diferença entre selador e fundo preparador?', 'O selador penetra e uniformiza a absorção, reduzindo o consumo de tinta. O fundo preparador cria uma camada de aderência sobre superfícies brilhantes ou pouco absorventes, como pintura antiga em bom estado.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-materials', 6, 'Tinta PVA ou acrílica?', 'A PVA é mais barata, tem menor resistência à lavagem e é indicada para ambientes internos secos, como quartos e salas. A acrílica custa mais, cobre melhor, resiste à umidade e à limpeza, e é a escolha para cozinhas, banheiros, áreas de serviço e ambientes muito usados.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-overview', 1, 'Por que o bloco cerâmico domina?', 'Porque é leve, barato, fácil de assentar e aceita bem o revestimento. O bloco cerâmico tem furos que reduzem peso e melhoram o isolamento térmico, e a face rugosa ajuda a aderência do chapisco.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-overview', 2, 'Qual a diferença entre chapisco, emboço e reboco?', 'Chapisco é a camada de aderência, fina e rugosa, aplicada direto sobre o bloco. Emboço é a camada de regularização, mais espessa, que corrige o prumo e a planeza. Reboco é a camada final, fina, que deixa a superfície pronta para a massa ou para a tinta.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-overview', 3, 'Por que a umidade é o assunto central?', 'Porque a maior parte dos defeitos de parede no Brasil passa por ela, direta ou indiretamente. A água entra por infiltração na fachada, por vazamento hidráulico, por umidade ascendente do solo, por chuva batendo em parede sem beiral suficiente, ou por condensação em ambientes fechados.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-overview', 4, 'Como a parede se comporta ao longo do ano?', 'Ela trabalha. A temperatura varia, o bloco dilata, o reboco retrai na cura, a estrutura do prédio se move. Fissuras capilares aparecem nos primeiros meses, e são normais. Fissuras maiores, inclinadas ou que aumentam com o tempo não são normais e precisam de diagnóstico.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-overview', 5, 'Quais revestimentos existem no Brasil?', 'A massa corrida, à base de PVA ou acrílica, aplicada em duas ou três demãos sobre o reboco, para corrigir pequenas imperfeições e uniformizar a absorção. A textura, aplicada com rolo ou desempenadeira, para dar relevo.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-overview', 6, 'O que decide a durabilidade de uma parede?', 'Três coisas, nesta ordem: a qualidade do reboco, a ausência de umidade e a preparação antes da tinta. O produto aparece em quarto lugar, e é onde a maioria das pessoas concentra a atenção e o orçamento.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-quality-checks', 1, 'Como verificar o prumo e a planeza?', 'Com régua de dois metros em várias direções, prumo e nível, e luz rasante. A tolerância depende do acabamento previsto: parede que receberá apenas pintura exige mais que parede que receberá revestimento cerâmico.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-quality-checks', 2, 'Como testar a aderência do reboco?', 'Batendo de leve com o cabo de uma ferramenta em toda a superfície e riscando com a unha em ponto discreto. Som oco indica descolamento; pó na unha indica revestimento fraco. Nenhum dos dois se corrige com massa ou tinta.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-quality-checks', 3, 'Como medir a umidade?', 'Com medidor em vários pontos e alturas, comparando com leitura anterior. Um valor isolado informa pouco; o que informa é a variação e a persistência. Parede que continua úmida semanas depois indica causa externa: infiltração, vazamento ou ventilação insuficiente.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-quality-checks', 4, 'O que verificar depois da massa?', 'A ausência de bolhas, a uniformidade da superfície ao lixamento e a inexistência de pontos brilhantes. Bolha indica umidade ou aplicação sobre superfície engordurada; ponto brilhante indica massa mal lixada, que vai marcar a tinta.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-quality-checks', 5, 'O que verificar depois da tinta?', 'A uniformidade de cor, a ausência de manchas de recobrimento, a nitidez dos recortes e a limpeza dos cantos. Mancha de recobrimento indica demãos desiguais ou aplicação sobre superfície com absorção diferente.'),
  ('br-brick-plaster-quality-checks', 6, 'Como registrar a vistoria?', 'Com fotos datadas, pontos numerados por ambiente e descrição objetiva do que foi encontrado. Registro vago não permite retrabalho localizado, e a discussão se arrasta sem conclusão.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-installation', 1, 'Como começar a marcação?', 'Com o projeto em mãos, marcando no piso e no teto as linhas da parede e conferindo o esquadro pela diagonal. Uma parede leve fora de esquadro desalinha todo o ambiente, e o erro não se corrige no acabamento.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-installation', 2, 'Como fixar as guias?', 'Com fixador compatível com o piso e com o teto, respeitando o espaçamento previsto, e com fita de vedação entre perfil e base. A fita não é opcional: além de vedar, ela desacopla a estrutura e melhora o desempenho acústico.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-installation', 3, 'Como posicionar os montantes?', 'Encaixados nas guias, no espaçamento definido em projeto, com o lado aberto voltado para a mesma direção para permitir a passagem de instalações. Montante invertido no meio da parede cria ponto de rigidez desigual e complica a fixação.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-installation', 4, 'O que deve acontecer antes de fechar a parede?', 'Toda a instalação: elétrica, hidráulica, dados e ar-condicionado, com testes de estanqueidade e de continuidade feitos e registrados. Fechar a parede com instalação incompleta significa reabrir depois, com perda de chapa, de tratamento e de pintura.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-installation', 5, 'Como parafusar a chapa?', 'Começando pelo centro e seguindo para as bordas, com parafusos perpendiculares, espaçados conforme norma e com a cabeça ligeiramente afundada sem romper o papel. A chapa deve ser apoiada de forma contínua nos perfis.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-installation', 6, 'Como tratar as juntas?', 'Em três etapas, com fita e massa específica: embutir a fita, cobrir e alisar, lixar levemente entre etapas. As juntas devem ficar levemente mais largas que a fita, para que a transição com a chapa seja gradual e não forme degrau.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-maintenance-repair', 1, 'Como corrigir trinca em junta?', 'Abrindo levemente a trinca, removendo massa solta, reaplicando fita e massa em três etapas e lixando. Correção com massa apenas, sem fita, reabre no primeiro movimento da estrutura, e a segunda ocorrência é sempre mais visível que a primeira.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-maintenance-repair', 2, 'Como tapar um furo?', 'Com massa de junta em duas demãos, lixamento leve e fundo antes da tinta. Furos de até alguns centímetros se resolvem assim. Furos maiores pedem recorte de chapa e reforço, porque massa em área extensa não tem resistência.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-maintenance-repair', 3, 'Como fixar objetos com segurança?', 'Localizando os montantes com detector, ou usando fixadores específicos para parede leve quando não há montante no ponto desejado. Quadro e prateleira leve não exigem mais que isso; televisão, armário e espelho grande exigem reforço interno.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-maintenance-repair', 4, 'O que fazer em caso de umidade?', 'Identificar a origem primeiro: vazamento, infiltração, condensação ou respingo constante. Depois, remover a área afetada até a chapa sadia, substituir o trecho comprometido por chapa RU e refazer o tratamento de juntas.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-maintenance-repair', 5, 'Como reparar uma aresta danificada?', 'Recortando a parte esfarelada, preenchendo com massa de reparo e reinstalando fita ou perfil de canto. A aresta é a zona de maior exposição e a que mais sofre em mudança e em obra dentro de casa.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-maintenance-repair', 6, 'Quando vale reparar e quando vale refazer o pano?', 'Repara quando o dano é localizado e a estrutura está íntegra. Refaz quando há umidade generalizada, várias trincas ativas ou dano estrutural. O critério é a origem única ou múltipla do problema.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-materials', 1, 'Como escolher o perfil?', 'Pela espessura da chapa de aço, pela geometria e pela função. Guias vão no piso e no teto; montantes são os elementos verticais; travamentos e reforços completam o conjunto. Perfis mais finos são adequados a paredes baixas e sem exigência acústica.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-materials', 2, 'Qual chapa usar: ST, RU ou RF?', 'A chapa standard (ST) serve para paredes comuns em ambientes secos. A resistente à umidade (RU) é obrigatória em banheiros, cozinhas e áreas molhadas. A resistente ao fogo (RF) é exigida em compartimentação e em rotas de fuga, conforme projeto.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-materials', 3, 'Quantas chapas por face?', 'Depende do desempenho exigido: uma chapa em paredes simples, duas ou mais em paredes que precisam de resistência a impacto, isolamento acústico ou resistência ao fogo. A espessura e o número de camadas constam de projeto e de norma.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-materials', 4, 'Que parafusos usar?', 'Parafusos auto-atarraxantes específicos para chapa de gesso, com comprimento compatível com a espessura da chapa e com a penetração no perfil. O passo entre parafusos é definido por norma e não deve ser aumentado para acelerar o serviço.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-materials', 5, 'Qual massa usar no tratamento de juntas?', 'Massa específica para juntas, com fita de reforço de papel ou fibra, aplicada em três etapas: embutimento da fita, cobertura e acabamento. Massa corrida comum não substitui a massa de junta, porque retrai de forma diferente e não acompanha o movimento.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-materials', 6, 'Quais acessórios importam?', 'Fita de vedação perimetral, massa acústica em pontos de contato, reforços metálicos para cargas e fitas de canto para arestas. Cada um resolve um ponto específico, e nenhum é decorativo.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-overview', 1, 'Por que usar estrutura metálica e não madeira?', 'Porque o perfil galvanizado não apodrece, não serve de alimento a cupim, não muda de dimensão com a umidade e tem comportamento previsível. Em país de clima úmido, essa estabilidade vale mais que o custo ligeiramente maior do material.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-overview', 2, 'Como a parede leve se comporta estruturalmente?', 'Ela não é estrutura do edifício, é vedação. Suporta o próprio peso, as cargas de fixação previstas e as ações de uso, e transmite tudo para o piso e para o teto. Confundir vedação com estrutura é o erro que produz acidentes e patologias.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-overview', 3, 'Quais são as vantagens reais?', 'Peso reduzido, execução rápida, ausência de argamassa, passagem de instalações dentro da cavidade e possibilidade de desmontar e remontar com pouca perda. Um banheiro ou uma parede divisória de escritório se resolve em dias, não em semanas.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-overview', 4, 'Quais são as limitações?', 'Resistência a impacto, isolamento acústico e capacidade de fixação são menores que os da alvenaria, a menos que o sistema seja dimensionado para isso. Quadro pesado, prateleira carregada e televisão exigem reforço interno previsto em projeto.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-overview', 5, 'Como se compara em custo?', 'O custo direto costuma ser competitivo, sobretudo quando se conta o tempo de obra. A economia aparece no prazo, na limpeza e na ausência de argamassa, e desaparece quando o sistema é mal dimensionado e exige retrabalho.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-overview', 6, 'Onde esse sistema não deve ser usado?', 'Em paredes externas sem sistema específico, em ambientes com umidade permanente sem chapa adequada, em locais de impacto severo sem reforço e em qualquer situação em que o projeto não preveja o sistema.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-quality-checks', 1, 'O que verificar antes de fechar a parede?', 'Instalações completas, montantes no espaçamento previsto, reforços nos pontos de carga, guias fixadas e fita perimetral instalada. Uma foto de cada pano de parede antes do fechamento resolve qualquer dúvida futura sobre o que existe dentro.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-quality-checks', 2, 'Como detectar estrutura inadequada?', 'Empurrando a parede com a mão e batendo com os nós dos dedos em pontos distribuídos. Parede firme não balança; som uniformemente oco sem variação brusca indica estrutura regular. Vibração excessiva indica espaçamento grande ou perfil fino.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-quality-checks', 3, 'Como conferir o tratamento de juntas?', 'Com luz rasante, apoiando um refletor no chão e olhando a superfície de lado. Junta mal tratada aparece como faixa com relevo, e é o defeito que mais compromete a percepção de qualidade de uma parede leve.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-quality-checks', 4, 'O que verificar nos cantos e arestas?', 'Alinhamento, presença de fita de canto e ausência de trinca. Arestas são a parte mais exposta da parede leve e a primeira a ser atingida por móveis, carrinhos e equipamentos. Sem reforço, a aresta se desfaz e a chapa exposta absorve umidade.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-quality-checks', 5, 'Como avaliar a superfície para a pintura?', 'Por absorção e por uniformidade. Passar a mão e observar pó, verificar se há diferença de textura entre chapa e massa, e testar uma área pequena com fundo. Absorção desigual produz mancha de recobrimento mesmo com tinta de boa qualidade.'),
  ('br-drywall-steel-stud-quality-checks', 6, 'Que registros devem ficar com o proprietário?', 'Fotos do interior da parede antes do fechamento, mapa dos reforços, especificação das chapas utilizadas e referência de cor e produto de pintura. Esse conjunto permite reformar, fixar e repintar sem demolir.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-installation', 1, 'How is the wall prepared for plaster?', 'The joints are raked or left rough, mortar droppings are removed, and the surface is brushed and wetted. On a smooth concrete or AAC surface the plaster needs a bonding agent or a spatterdash coat rather than being spread directly.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-installation', 2, 'How thick is the backing coat?', 'Twelve to fifteen millimetres for internal walls, fifteen to twenty for external. Where the wall is out of tolerance, the backing coat is built up in layers of no more than ten millimetres each, with the previous layer set before the next.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-installation', 3, 'How is the backing coat keyed?', 'With a scratching tool or a stiff brush, dragged across the surface while the mortar is green, producing grooves that the finishing coat will grip. A smooth backing coat is a debonded finishing coat waiting to happen.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-installation', 4, 'When does the finishing coat go on?', 'After the backing coat has set but while it is still green, usually the next day, having been wetted first. A finishing coat on a fully dried backing coat needs extra wetting and a bonding coat to stay put.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-installation', 5, 'How long is curing?', 'Seven days minimum, fourteen better, wetting the wall two or three times a day and keeping it damp rather than flooding it once. In hot dry weather the plaster can lose its water faster than the cement can use it.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-installation', 6, 'How dry must the wall be before putty?', 'Dry enough that a moisture meter reads low and consistently, and that the surface looks uniformly pale. In practice that is a few weeks after curing in dry weather, and longer in the monsoon.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-maintenance', 1, 'When is repair needed rather than repainting?', 'When the surface is no longer sound: hollow plaster, cracks that move, damp that returns, or a paint film that lifts rather than chalks. Repainting over any of those buys months rather than years.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-maintenance', 2, 'How is hollow plaster repaired?', 'By cutting out the hollow area to sound plaster with square edges, cleaning and wetting the substrate, applying a bonding coat, then filling in layers no thicker than ten millimetres and allowing each to set.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-maintenance', 3, 'How are cracks repaired?', 'By opening to a V, cleaning out, priming, filling with a flexible filler, and reinforcing wide or recurring cracks with a mesh strip bedded in the filler before the finishing coats.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-maintenance', 4, 'How is damp dealt with?', 'By finding the source before touching the surface. Check the roof, the parapet, the window, the sill, the plumbing and the external render in that order, then repair the source, then allow the wall to dry, then repair the plaster.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-maintenance', 5, 'What about salt damage?', 'Chop back to sound material, dry brush the salt off, allow the wall to dry thoroughly, and replaster with a mix that resists salt, or with a renovation plaster designed for the purpose.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-maintenance', 6, 'How is the old paint film prepared?', 'Washed to remove chalk, scraped where loose, sanded where glossy, and spot-primed where bare. A film that is chalked but intact can be repainted after washing; a film that is flaking has to come off where it is failing.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-materials', 1, 'Which blocks or bricks should be used?', 'Fired clay bricks, fly ash bricks, solid or hollow concrete blocks, or AAC blocks, each to its own Indian standard. For interior walls the important properties are dimensional consistency, strength and, for AAC, water absorption.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-materials', 2, 'What cement grade is right?', 'Ordinary Portland cement of the grade specified, most commonly 43 or 53 grade for structural work, and in practice the same cement is used for plaster. What matters more than grade is freshness: cement older than three months, or cement that has taken moisture, has lost strength that cannot be recovered.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-materials', 3, 'How is sand assessed?', 'By gradation and by silt content. Well graded sand with a range of particle sizes produces a dense, workable mortar; uniformly fine sand needs more cement and shrinks more. The field test is the bottle test: sand and water shaken and left to settle, with the silt layer visible as a distinct band.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-materials', 4, 'What about water?', 'Potable water, free of salts, acids, organic matter and visible contamination. On many Indian sites the water used for concrete is clean and the water used for plaster is whatever is in the drum, which is how a clean wall ends up with efflorescence.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-materials', 5, 'What putty should be used?', 'A ready-mix acrylic or polymer-modified wall putty, applied in two thin coats and sanded between them. The cement-based powders are cheaper and harder to sand; the acrylic ones grip better and produce a smoother base for paint.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-materials', 6, 'Which primer is needed?', 'An alkali-resistant primer on fresh plaster, because cement plaster stays alkaline for months and an ordinary primer will not block it. On repairs and previously painted surfaces, a penetrating or bonding primer matched to the existing coating.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-overview', 1, 'What is the wall made of?', 'Brick or concrete block masonry, a cement-sand plaster in two coats, a thin skim of wall putty, an alkali-resistant primer and an emulsion finish. That is the whole stack, and it is the same whether the final colour is a pale ivory or a deep accent.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-overview', 2, 'Why does plaster matter so much?', 'Because it is the layer that receives everything else. A plaster that is flat, sound and properly cured gives putty and paint a stable base. A plaster that is friable, cracked or too thick guarantees that the next two layers will fail even if they are applied perfectly.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-overview', 3, 'How do the layers interact?', 'Mechanically and chemically. The plaster keys into the roughened masonry face; the putty grips the plaster''s pores; the primer penetrates the putty and blocks alkali; the paint bonds to the primed surface.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-overview', 4, 'Where do these walls usually fail?', 'In this order: shrinkage cracks in the plaster, hollow or debonded plaster, putty blistering over damp patches, paint peeling over inadequate primer, and damp staining through an external wall.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-overview', 5, 'What decides the quality of the finish?', 'Three things: the flatness of the masonry, the thickness and cure of the plaster, and the moisture content before putty. Get those right and a standard emulsion looks excellent. Get them wrong and the most expensive paint in the shop looks like a patch job.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-overview', 6, 'How long does the finish last?', 'Six to ten years for an interior emulsion on a sound plaster in a dry room, less in a bathroom or a kitchen where humidity is high, and less again where the wall is on a damp external face.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-quality', 1, 'How is plaster flatness measured?', 'With a two-metre straight edge laid in several directions on each wall, and a rule to measure any gap beneath it. A gap of more than about three millimetres in two metres is outside normal Indian tolerance for a painted finish.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-quality', 2, 'How is soundness tested?', 'By tapping with a knuckle or a light hammer across the surface and listening for a hollow note. Hollow patches mean the plaster has debonded from the masonry, and they will eventually fall or telegraph through the paint as a blister.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-quality', 3, 'How are cracks recorded?', 'By width, length, position and orientation, with a date. A hairline crack following a material joint is a different finding from a two millimetre crack crossing bricks, and the record is what allows the next inspection to see whether it is moving.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-quality', 4, 'How is moisture measured before painting?', 'With a moisture meter, on several points per wall, and on a fresh break where possible. The reading should be low and uniform, and any patch reading significantly higher than its surroundings indicates trapped moisture rather than a damp day.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-quality', 5, 'How is putty quality judged?', 'By the surface it leaves after sanding: uniform, opaque, with no trowel ridges and no sanding-through to plaster. Thin areas show as darker patches that the primer will not hide. Putty that powders heavily when sanded has been applied too dry or is of low binder content.'),
  ('brick-block-plaster-quality', 6, 'How is film build checked?', 'By measuring the wet film with a comb gauge during application, or the dry film with an electronic gauge afterwards. The data sheet gives the wet and dry figures for the product, and those figures are the specification.'),
  ('brick-veneer-materials', 1, 'Which brick should be used?', 'The brick should be rated for the exposure, which in Australian practice means a durability classification matched to the site. Bricks are graded for salt attack and for thermal movement, and a coastal site needs a higher grade than a sheltered inland one.'),
  ('brick-veneer-materials', 2, 'What do veneer ties do and which type?', 'Veneer ties connect the brick skin to the frame, transfer wind load, and allow small movement between the two. Ties are made of galvanised or stainless steel, and the material is chosen for the exposure: stainless on coastal work, heavily galvanised inland.'),
  ('brick-veneer-materials', 3, 'Why does sarking specification matter?', 'Sarking specification matters because it is the layer that keeps the frame dry and still lets the wall breathe. The product is a vapour-permeable membrane with a water-resistance rating, and it is installed with lapped joints running so that water drains outward and downward.'),
  ('brick-veneer-materials', 4, 'What flashings are needed?', 'Flashings are needed at every opening, at the base of the wall, at roof junctions and wherever the cavity is interrupted. A flashing collects water from the cavity and directs it out through weep holes or over a drip edge.'),
  ('brick-veneer-materials', 5, 'Which plasterboard thickness?', 'Plasterboard thickness is chosen for the wall''s performance: 10 mm is common on walls, 13 mm where impact resistance, fire rating or a better acoustic result is required. Thicker board is stiffer, heavier and better at resisting a blow, which matters in corridors and family rooms.'),
  ('brick-veneer-materials', 6, 'Which jointing compound and level?', 'Jointing compound is chosen with the level of finish, and the level is stated in the contract. A level that requires taping, three coats and full sanding needs a compound that sands to a feather edge without tearing.'),
  ('brick-veneer-plasterboard-overview', 1, 'What is a brick veneer wall?', 'A brick veneer wall is a single skin of clay brick tied to a timber or steel frame, with a drained cavity between them and the frame lined internally with plasterboard. The brick is non-structural cladding: it carries its own weight onto the footing and transfers lateral wind load back to the frame through ties.'),
  ('brick-veneer-plasterboard-overview', 2, 'Why does Australia build this way?', 'Australia builds this way because clay brick is cheap and durable there, timber framing is fast, and plasterboard gives a flat interior at low cost. The combination suits the climate and the labour market: bricklaying is a trade with a deep local base, timber framing is quick on a cleared site, and the finished wall performs well against sun and rain without any coating on the brick.'),
  ('brick-veneer-plasterboard-overview', 3, 'What does each layer do?', 'Each layer has one job, and the system only performs when each is done. The brick skin sheds rain and provides the visible facade. The cavity drains any water that gets behind the brick and provides a break between the wet outer skin and the frame.'),
  ('brick-veneer-plasterboard-overview', 4, 'How wide should the cavity be?', 'The cavity should be wide enough to drain and to ventilate, and it is specified rather than left to chance. A nominal 40 mm cavity is common in Australian residential work, with a minimum stated in the code and in the masonry standard.'),
  ('brick-veneer-plasterboard-overview', 5, 'How does water actually behave in this wall?', 'Water enters the brick skin through wind-driven rain, travels down the back of the brick, and leaves through weep holes at the base. That is the design assumption: the brick is a screen, not a seal.'),
  ('brick-veneer-plasterboard-overview', 6, 'What is sarking for?', 'Sarking is the water-resistant, vapour-permeable membrane fixed to the outside of the frame, and it does two jobs. It keeps wind-driven rain off the timber and it lets vapour escape from the inside of the house, which is what prevents condensation in the frame.'),
  ('building-brick-veneer-plasterboard-wall', 1, 'How is the frame prepared?', 'The frame is built, straightened and checked for plumb before anything is fixed to its outside face. Straightness matters because the plasterboard will reproduce whatever the frame does, and because the brick skin needs a consistent cavity.'),
  ('building-brick-veneer-plasterboard-wall', 2, 'How is sarking installed?', 'Sarking is fixed to the outside of the frame with laps running so water drains outward and downward, and with the joints taped as specified. The laps are the whole point. A membrane lapped the wrong way channels water into the frame instead of away from it, and that error is invisible once the brick is up.'),
  ('building-brick-veneer-plasterboard-wall', 3, 'When do flashings go in?', 'Flashings go in before the brick reaches them, and they must be in place before any brick is laid over the opening. Base flashings at the foot of the wall, sill flashings at openings, head flashings above them and the flashing at a roof junction are all installed as the brickwork rises, not retrofitted.'),
  ('building-brick-veneer-plasterboard-wall', 4, 'How is the brick skin built?', 'The brick skin is laid on the footing, tied to the frame as it rises, and kept clear of the frame by the cavity. Ties are fixed to the frame at the specified spacing and embedded in the mortar joint as the brickwork reaches them.'),
  ('building-brick-veneer-plasterboard-wall', 5, 'How is the cavity kept clear?', 'The cavity is kept clear by a cavity batten, by careful mortar handling and by cleaning as the work rises. A batten pulled up as the brickwork rises lifts droppings out with it rather than letting them accumulate at the base.'),
  ('building-brick-veneer-plasterboard-wall', 6, 'How is the plasterboard fixed?', 'Plasterboard is fixed after the roof is on and the building is weatherproof, using adhesive, screws or both as specified. Sheets are set out so that joints land on framing and do not align with the joints on the other side of the wall.'),
  ('checking-alc-panel-wall-quality', 1, 'How do you check the panel plane?', 'The panel plane is checked against a straightedge and a line, and against the frame survey rather than against the previous panel. A two-metre straightedge laid across the joints shows the local plane; a line or laser along the facade shows the overall plane.'),
  ('checking-alc-panel-wall-quality', 2, 'What makes a good joint?', 'A good joint has the specified width, a backer rod at the specified depth, sealant adhered to both faces and tooled to a concave profile. Width and depth are measured rather than estimated.'),
  ('checking-alc-panel-wall-quality', 3, 'How is sealant adhesion tested?', 'Adhesion is tested by hand and, on a building, by a peel test on a sample joint. The hand test is a thumb press at the joint edge: a bonded sealant resists, a failed one lifts. A peel test cuts a short section of sealant free at one face and pulls it back to see whether the failure is adhesive, at the panel, or cohesive, within the sealant itself.'),
  ('checking-alc-panel-wall-quality', 4, 'How is coating thickness verified?', 'Coating thickness is verified by reading the application rate and by measuring on a sample area. Japanese systems state a wet or dry film thickness, or a coverage rate per coat, and the applied number of coats is checked against the specification.'),
  ('checking-alc-panel-wall-quality', 5, 'What are the anchor and fixing checks?', 'Every fixing is checked for corrosion, for coating continuity around it and for whether the panel is cracked at the connection. A rust stain on an ALC facade is a fixing that has lost its protection, and the crack that follows is a permanent water path.'),
  ('checking-alc-panel-wall-quality', 6, 'How do you check for water behind the facade?', 'Water behind an ALC facade is found by looking indoors at the stains and outdoors at the joints above them. Indoors, the evidence is a damp patch, a tide mark or mould at a level that does not correspond to a window.'),
  ('checking-brick-veneer-plasterboard-wall', 1, 'How is cavity width checked?', 'Cavity width is checked at several heights on each elevation, against the specified nominal width and its tolerance. The check is a tape or a purpose-made gauge inserted through a weep hole or a temporarily open joint, recorded at the base, mid-height and top.'),
  ('checking-brick-veneer-plasterboard-wall', 2, 'How is the cavity checked for mortar?', 'The cavity is checked for mortar droppings at each lift, before the next lift covers them. The test is visual through the open top of the cavity at each lift and by feeling for obstructions.'),
  ('checking-brick-veneer-plasterboard-wall', 3, 'How are ties checked?', 'Ties are checked for density, for fixing to the frame and for embedment in the mortar joint. Density is a count against the specified spacing per square metre. Fixing is a pull test on a sample, and embedment is visual as the brickwork rises.'),
  ('checking-brick-veneer-plasterboard-wall', 4, 'How are sarking laps checked?', 'Sarking laps are checked for direction, overlap and taping before the brick reaches them. The lap direction must shed water outward and downward. The overlap must match the specification.'),
  ('checking-brick-veneer-plasterboard-wall', 5, 'How are weep holes and flashings checked?', 'Weep holes are checked for presence, spacing and clearance; flashings are checked for position, lap and discharge. A weep hole is only useful if it is open and if the flashing behind it directs water to it.'),
  ('checking-brick-veneer-plasterboard-wall', 6, 'How is plasterboard fixing checked?', 'Plasterboard fixing is checked for the fixing pattern, for joints landing on framing, and for the level of finish achieved. Fixings are counted against the specified pattern, and the board is pressed to find hollow spots where adhesive has not taken.'),
  ('checking-coated-alc-facade', 1, 'How is film thickness verified?', 'Film thickness is verified by material consumption against area and, on a sample, by a gauge. The arithmetic is the primary check: litres used divided by area coated gives a film build, and it is compared with the specification.'),
  ('checking-coated-alc-facade', 2, 'How is adhesion tested?', 'Adhesion is tested by a cross-cut or a pull-off test on a representative area, and by hand at the edges of the work. A cross-cut reads the way the film releases: clean squares indicate good adhesion, ragged or partial release indicates a weak bond.'),
  ('checking-coated-alc-facade', 3, 'What does good joint bridging look like?', 'Good joint bridging is a continuous film over the joint with no crack, no line and no change of sheen along the sealant. The joint is checked with a straightedge and with raking light.'),
  ('checking-coated-alc-facade', 4, 'How is continuity at fixings checked?', 'Each fixing is checked as a penetration: the coating should pass over it without a break, a pinhole or a thin edge. A fixing is where a facade leaks, because it is a small discontinuity with a metal core that corrodes when wet.'),
  ('checking-coated-alc-facade', 5, 'How is colour uniformity judged?', 'Colour uniformity is judged on a completed elevation, in daylight, from a distance, and against the approved sample. Patchiness comes from three causes: uneven primer, uneven film build, and application in two sessions with a lap between them.'),
  ('checking-coated-alc-facade', 6, 'What moisture checks apply to a coating?', 'Before coating, the moisture content of the panel decides whether the work can proceed; after coating, moisture readings indicate whether water is getting in. A panel that is damp traps water under the film, and the coating blisters or lifts as the moisture moves.'),
  ('checking-painted-japanese-wall', 1, 'How do you check that a joint is invisible?', 'A joint is checked with a straightedge and with raking light, on the sealed surface before the top coat goes on. The straightedge shows the local step. The raking light, held low and to one side, shows the shadow of any ridge, and it is the test that matches how the wall will look in a room with a window on one side.'),
  ('checking-painted-japanese-wall', 2, 'What does even sheen tell you?', 'Even sheen tells you the sealer worked and the paint was applied to a consistent film. Patchy sheen is caused by uneven absorption, which is a sealer problem, or by uneven application, which is a technique problem.'),
  ('checking-painted-japanese-wall', 3, 'How is film build checked?', 'Film build is checked against the stated number of coats and against the product''s coverage rate, with a physical check on a sample. The arithmetic is simple: litres used divided by area, compared with the product''s stated coverage.'),
  ('checking-painted-japanese-wall', 4, 'What about screw heads?', 'Screw heads are checked like joints: by light and by hand. A head that was not dimpled enough shows as a small bump, and a head that was over-filled and under-sanded shows as a small dish.'),
  ('checking-painted-japanese-wall', 5, 'How are edge lines judged?', 'Edge lines at ceilings, frames and corners are judged for straightness and for sharpness. A straight line is one that does not wander by more than a couple of millimetres over a wall''s length; a sharp line has no bleed onto the adjacent surface.'),
  ('checking-painted-japanese-wall', 6, 'What moisture checks matter after painting?', 'After painting, the moisture checks are about the wall behind the paint rather than the paint itself. A painted wall in a Japanese bathroom that blisters or lifts is telling you about moisture moving through the board, not about the paint.'),
  ('coating-alc-facade-elastic-paint-overview', 1, 'Why does ALC need a coating at all?', 'ALC needs a coating because the panel is porous and would otherwise absorb rain. The material is cellular concrete with an open pore structure. Water does not pass through it quickly, but it does soak into the surface, and a soaked panel is a panel that will release moisture inward, stain, grow mould and lose insulation performance.'),
  ('coating-alc-facade-elastic-paint-overview', 2, 'What does elastic mean in practice?', 'Elastic means the coating can stretch with the joint and the panel without splitting, and it is tested as the ability to bridge a moving crack. The movement comes from two sources: thermal movement of the panel and structural movement of the frame.'),
  ('coating-alc-facade-elastic-paint-overview', 3, 'Why do the joints decide the outcome?', 'The joints decide because they are the only part of the facade designed to move, and a coating fails where the thing beneath it moves. A sealed joint has a flexible sealant behind the coating.'),
  ('coating-alc-facade-elastic-paint-overview', 4, 'What is the coating system made of?', 'A typical system is a sealer or primer on the prepared panel, a flexible base coat that bridges the joints, and a top coat that provides weather resistance and colour. The sealer equalises absorption so the base coat does not dry unevenly.'),
  ('coating-alc-facade-elastic-paint-overview', 5, 'How thick does the coating need to be?', 'Thick enough to bridge the joint movement, which is stated as a dry film thickness or an application rate per square metre. Thickness is the difference between an elastic coating and a coat of coloured water.'),
  ('coating-alc-facade-elastic-paint-overview', 6, 'What are the failure points on a coated ALC facade?', 'Five points fail in a consistent order: the joint line, the fixings, the window reveals, the parapet and the corner. The joint fails first because it moves. The fixings fail second because each one is a penetration where the coating has to be continuous.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-installation', 1, 'Comment tracer une cloison ?', 'Au sol et au plafond, avec un cordeau et un niveau, en vérifiant l''aplomb et l''équerrage avec les murs existants. Le tracé conditionne tout : une cloison tracée de travers reste de travers, et aucun réglage ne la rattrape.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-installation', 2, 'Comment fixer les rails ?', 'Avec des fixations adaptées au support, espacées selon les règles de l''art, et une bande de désolidarisation en périphérie quand elle est prescrite. La désolidarisation évite que la cloison ne transmette les bruits de structure.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-installation', 3, 'Comment monter les montants ?', 'Ils sont glissés entre les rails, à l''entraxe prescrit, et laissés légèrement libres en tête pour permettre le jeu. Les montants sont ensuite alignés et vissés dans les rails, sans blocage rigide qui empêcherait la cloison de travailler.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-installation', 4, 'Quand passer les gaines ?', 'Avant la fermeture, toujours. Les gaines électriques, les réseaux de communication et parfois la plomberie traversent la cavité. Chaque traversée reçoit un fourreau, et chaque boîtier est fixé sur un montant ou sur un renfort dédié.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-installation', 5, 'Comment poser l''isolant ?', 'En le découpant légèrement plus large que la cavité, pour qu''il tienne seul, sans le comprimer. Il doit remplir toute la hauteur et toute la longueur, sans vide en périphérie. Un isolant posé à la va-vite, avec des vides en pied, laisse passer le son par les fentes.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-installation', 6, 'Comment fermer la cloison ?', 'En commençant par une face, puis en remplissant, puis en fermant la seconde. Les plaques sont posées en quinconce, décalées d''un montant, pour que les joints ne se superposent pas d''une face à l''autre.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-maintenance-repair', 1, 'Comment réparer un trou dans une cloison ?', 'Selon sa taille. Un petit trou se rebouche à l''enduit, en deux passes. Un trou moyen reçoit une pastille de plâtre collée. Une ouverture large impose de découper un morceau de plaque, de poser un renfort et de reprendre le joint.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-maintenance-repair', 2, 'Comment traiter une fissure ?', 'En identifiant son origine. Une fissure verticale au droit d''une porte vient d''un joint rigide. Une fissure horizontale en tête vient d''un montant bloqué. Une fissure en étoile vient d''un choc ou d''une fixation traversante.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-maintenance-repair', 3, 'Que faire d''un angle ébréché ?', 'Le reconstruire avec un enduit de rebouchage, puis le protéger par une cornière si l''angle est exposé. Sur un angle très sollicité, un profilé de protection évite la récidive. C''est une réparation de dix minutes qui, faite à temps, évite de reprendre un pan entier quelques années plus tard.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-maintenance-repair', 4, 'Comment réparer après un dégât des eaux ?', 'En attendant le séchage complet, puis en remplaçant les plaques atteintes. Une plaque mouillée perd sa rigidité et reste marquée ; la repeindre ne règle rien. Le contrôle se fait à l''humidimètre et au toucher.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-maintenance-repair', 5, 'Comment repeindre une cloison réparée ?', 'Avec un primaire sur les zones reprises, puis deux couches sur l''ensemble du pan, jamais sur la seule reprise. La différence d''absorption entre le plâtre neuf et l''ancienne peinture se voit en pleine lumière.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-maintenance-repair', 6, 'Que peut-on suspendre et comment ?', 'Une charge légère avec une cheville à bascule. Une charge lourde uniquement sur un renfort, ou avec une fixation traversante qui reporte la charge sur l''ossature. La limite n''est pas la plaque mais le montant.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-materials', 1, 'Quels profils pour quelle cloison ?', 'Deux familles : les rails, horizontaux, fixés au sol et au plafond, et les montants, verticaux, glissés entre les rails. Le profil courant a une largeur de quarante-huit, soixante-dix ou quatre-vingt-dix millimètres selon la performance recherchée.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-materials', 2, 'Quelles épaisseurs de tôle ?', 'Les profils courants sont en tôle de six à sept dixièmes de millimètre. Les profilés renforcés, plus épais, s''emploient quand la cloison doit reprendre des charges ou atteindre une performance acoustique supérieure.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-materials', 3, 'Quelle plaque pour quelle pièce ?', 'Une plaque standard en pièces sèches. Une plaque hydrofuge en salle de bain, en cuisine et derrière un plan de travail. Une plaque à hautes performances en pièce humide très sollicitée, comme une douche à l''italienne.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-materials', 4, 'Quel isolant mettre entre les montants ?', 'De la laine minérale, en panneaux ou en rouleaux, à la densité adaptée à la performance recherchée. L''isolant remplit la cavité sans être comprimé : une laine tassée perd une grande partie de son efficacité acoustique et thermique.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-materials', 5, 'À quoi servent les bandes et l''enduit à joint ?', 'À rendre la surface continue. Les bandes pontent les joints entre plaques, l''enduit noie les bandes et les têtes de vis. Trois passes successives, avec ponçage entre certaines, donnent une surface prête à peindre.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-materials', 6, 'Quels accessoires oublie-t-on le plus souvent ?', 'Quatre : les renforts pour charges lourdes, les cales de désolidarisation en périphérie, les profilés d''angle pour les arêtes saillantes et les fourreaux de traversée pour les gaines.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-overview', 1, 'Pourquoi une ossature métallique plutôt que du bois ?', 'Parce que l''acier ne travaille pas avec l''humidité. Un montant bois gonfle et se déforme dans un logement humide, un montant acier reste droit. L''acier est aussi incombustible, ce qui simplifie le traitement du feu dans les logements collectifs.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-overview', 2, 'Quelles plaques trouve-t-on en France ?', 'La plaque de plâtre standard, dite BA13 pour son épaisseur de treize millimètres, couvre la majorité des cloisons intérieures. La BA10, plus fine, s''emploie en doublage ou en plafond.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-overview', 3, 'Que disent les règles de l''art ?', 'Les DTU relatifs aux ouvrages en plaques de plâtre fixent les entraxes de montants, les fixations, les joints, les tolérances de planéité et les règles de désolidarisation. Ces documents sont la référence contractuelle, et une entreprise qui s''en écarte sans justification s''expose à refuser la réception.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-overview', 4, 'Pourquoi l''entraxe des montants est-il décisif ?', 'Parce qu''il conditionne tout le reste : la rigidité de la paroi, la résistance aux chocs, la tenue des fixations et le comportement acoustique. Un entraxe large fait une cloison souple qui vibre et qui se déforme quand on s''y appuie.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-overview', 5, 'Comment se comporte la cloison face au bruit ?', 'Elle sépare deux pièces, et c''est son rôle le plus mal compris. Une cloison simple peau n''isole presque rien. Une cloison double peau avec laine minérale isole correctement. Entre les deux, il y a toute la gamme des performances, et un écart de prix beaucoup plus faible que l''écart de confort.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-overview', 6, 'Que peut-on fixer sur une cloison ?', 'Une charge légère, oui, avec des chevilles adaptées. Une charge lourde, uniquement si un renfort a été prévu au montage. Une télévision, une bibliothèque ou un meuble suspendu exigent une plaque de renfort ou une ossature doublée, posées avant la fermeture.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-quality-checks', 1, 'Comment contrôler l''aplomb et l''équerrage ?', 'Au niveau et à l''équerre, sur toute la hauteur, et par la méthode trois-quatre-cinq pour les grandes dimensions. Un défaut d''aplomb se répercute sur la pose des meubles, des portes et des plinthes.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-quality-checks', 2, 'Comment mesurer la planéité ?', 'Avec une règle de deux mètres posée dans plusieurs directions, complétée par un relevé sous lumière rasante. Les tolérances dépendent de la finition annoncée et du type de cloison.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-quality-checks', 3, 'Comment vérifier la tenue des fixations ?', 'En appuyant fermement sur la plaque, entre deux montants, et en vérifiant qu''aucune vis ne ressort ni ne tourne dans le vide. Une vis qui tourne indique un montant absent ou mal aligné.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-quality-checks', 4, 'Comment contrôler les joints ?', 'En les regardant sous lumière rasante, à contre-jour, et en passant la main perpendiculairement. Un joint correctement traité ne se sent pas et ne se voit pas. Les défauts classiques sont la surépaisseur, la bande mal noyée et le ponçage insuffisant.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-quality-checks', 5, 'Que regarder aux angles et aux portes ?', 'Les arêtes saillantes, qui doivent être protégées, et les jeux autour des huisseries, qui doivent rester souples. Un joint rigide au droit d''une porte fissure en quelques mois. C''est le point de reprise le plus fréquent sur une cloison neuve.'),
  ('fr-drywall-metal-stud-quality-checks', 6, 'Comment vérifier l''acoustique ?', 'Difficilement en visite, sauf à tester une pièce voisine en fonctionnement. Les indices de performance figurent dans les documents du marché, et le contrôle porte alors sur la conformité de ce qui a été posé : isolant, épaisseur, désolidarisation.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-installation', 1, 'Pourquoi commencer par un diagnostic ?', 'Parce que le choix des produits dépend entièrement de l''état du support. Un enduit qui sonne creux, un support farinant, une maçonnerie humide et un enduit sain n''appellent pas le même traitement, et un devis établi sans sondage est un devis sur photographie.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-installation', 2, 'Comment nettoyer une façade ?', 'Par lavage à basse pression, avec un produit adapté au type d''encrassement : pollution, mousses, algues, efflorescences. Le nettoyage haute pression est à éviter sur les enduits anciens, qu''il creuse et déstructure.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-installation', 3, 'Comment réparer avant de finir ?', 'En purgeant les parties non adhérentes, en rebouchant les fissures selon leur nature et en reprenant les points singuliers. Un rebouchage rigide sur une fissure active ne tient pas une saison, alors qu''un produit souple accompagne le mouvement.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-installation', 4, 'Comment préparer la surface ?', 'Par dépoussiérage soigné, traitement des sels résiduels, et application d''un primaire adapté. La poussière est le premier ennemi de l''adhérence : elle est invisible sur une façade grise et suffit à faire décolller une peinture en quelques années.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-installation', 5, 'Quand faut-il un échafaudage ?', 'Dès que la surface dépasse ce qu''un particulier peut atteindre en sécurité, ce qui est presque toujours le cas sur une façade complète. Les travaux en hauteur sur échelle ou sur nacelle individuelle multiplient les risques et dégradent la qualité d''exécution.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-installation', 6, 'Combien de couches ?', 'Un primaire et deux couches de finition sur un support préparé et homogène. Une sous-couche teintée réduit le nombre de couches sur les changements de teinte marqués, mais ne remplace jamais le primaire.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-maintenance-repair', 1, 'À quelle fréquence nettoyer une façade ?', 'Tous les trois à cinq ans dans un environnement urbain ou exposé aux embruns, tous les cinq à huit ans à la campagne. Le nettoyage n''est pas esthétique : il retire les dépôts qui retiennent l''humidité contre l''enduit.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-maintenance-repair', 2, 'Comment traiter les mousses et les algues ?', 'En traitant d''abord la cause : humidité persistante, ombrage, gouttière qui déborde, pied de mur qui reçoit les projections. Un anti-mousse appliqué seul revient en deux saisons, au même endroit et souvent plus largement.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-maintenance-repair', 3, 'Que faire des fissures ?', 'Les classer avant de les traiter. Une microfissure de retrait se rebouche souplement. Une fissure de plusieurs millimètres, qui suit un joint de maçonnerie ou s''ouvre avec les saisons, demande un diagnostic.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-maintenance-repair', 4, 'Comment traiter les efflorescences ?', 'Les efflorescences sont des sels qui cristallisent en surface quand l''eau traverse la maçonnerie. Les brosser sans traiter la cause les fait revenir, parfois plus abondantes. La cause est une humidité qui circule : remontée capillaire, infiltration, ou enduit trop étanche.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-maintenance-repair', 5, 'Quand faut-il reprendre localement ou globalement ?', 'Localement quand le désordre est ponctuel et la cause traitée. Globalement quand plus d''un tiers de la surface est dégradé, ou quand les reprises successives ont créé des différences de teinte et d''absorption visibles de la rue.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-maintenance-repair', 6, 'Comment entretenir une façade en pierre ?', 'Avec des produits adaptés au calcaire ou au granit, et sans jamais appliquer de produit filmogène. La pierre se nettoie par lavage doux, par brossage et, si nécessaire, par des compresses absorbantes sur les zones tachées.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-materials', 1, 'Enduit à la chaux ou au ciment ?', 'La chaux est souple, perméable et tolérante : elle convient au bâti ancien, aux murs de pierre et aux supports qui bougent. Le ciment est plus dur, plus résistant mécaniquement, moins perméable : il convient aux maçonneries modernes stables.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-materials', 2, 'Comment reconnaître un enduit incompatible ?', 'Par des signes simples : décollements en plaques, cloquage uniforme sur une surface, fissures qui suivent les limites de l''enduit ancien, efflorescences récurrentes. Ces désordres apparaissent souvent dans l''année qui suit un ravalement.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-materials', 3, 'Quel primaire pour une façade ?', 'Un primaire d''accrochage sur un support peu absorbant ou irrégulier, un primaire de fond sur un enduit neuf très absorbant, et un primaire isolant sur une zone tachée. Sur façade, la poussière et les sels rendent le dépoussiérage et le traitement préalable indispensables.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-materials', 4, 'Quelle peinture pour une façade ?', 'Une peinture de façade microporeuse, adaptée au support minéral, résistante aux intempéries et à l''encrassement. Les produits dits « siloxane » ou « pliolite » offrent une bonne perméabilité à la vapeur avec une tenue correcte à la pluie.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-materials', 5, 'Faut-il choisir un revêtement souple ou une peinture ?', 'Le revêtement souple, plus épais, masque les microfissures et résiste mieux aux mouvements. La peinture, plus fine, conserve la texture du support et coûte moins cher à la pose. Le choix dépend de l''état du support.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-materials', 6, 'Quel rôle jouent les accessoires ?', 'Les joints, les appuis de fenêtre, les bandes de rive et les goutte-d''eau évitent que l''eau ne pénètre aux points singuliers. Ce sont eux qui décident du vieillissement d''une façade autant que le produit de surface.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-overview', 1, 'Que doit faire un enduit de façade ?', 'Trois choses, dans cet ordre : protéger la maçonnerie des agressions, permettre l''évacuation de la vapeur d''eau, et donner une surface régulière et esthétique. Un enduit qui remplit les deux premières fonctions mais pas la troisième reste un enduit correct ; l''inverse n''existe pas.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-overview', 2, 'Comment se comporte une façade au fil des saisons ?', 'Elle travaille. Les matériaux se dilatent au soleil et se contractent la nuit, l''humidité gonfle les supports, le gel fait éclater l''eau piégée dans les pores. Une façade neuve connaît son premier hiver comme une épreuve, et c''est souvent à ce moment qu''apparaissent les premières fissures.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-overview', 3, 'Quelles finitions trouve-t-on en France ?', 'L''enduit traditionnel à la chaux ou au ciment, appliqué en plusieurs couches, avec une finition grattée, talochée ou lissée. L''enduit monocouche, projeté et taloché, très répandu dans la construction neuve.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-overview', 4, 'Pourquoi l''humidité est-elle le vrai sujet ?', 'Parce que neuf désordres de façade sur dix en découlent, directement ou non. L''eau entre par capillarité, par fissure, par un joint défaillant, par une remontée depuis le sol, ou se condense à l''intérieur.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-overview', 5, 'Que change l''exposition ?', 'Tout. Une façade nord reste humide plus longtemps et sèche mal ; une façade sud subit le rayonnement et les écarts de température ; une façade ouest prend la pluie battante. Sur un même bâtiment, quatre murs peuvent nécessiter quatre traitements différents.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-overview', 6, 'Combien de temps dure une façade ?', 'Un enduit traditionnel correctement mis en œuvre tient vingt à trente ans, parfois davantage dans un climat tempéré. Un enduit monocouche en tient quinze à vingt. Une peinture de façade tient sept à douze ans selon l''exposition et la préparation.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-quality-checks', 1, 'Comment tester l''adhérence ?', 'Par sondage au marteau ou au manche d''outil, sur des zones réparties, et par un test de grattage sur une partie discrète. Un son clair et mat indique une bonne adhérence ; un son creux signale un décollement.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-quality-checks', 2, 'Comment vérifier l''homogénéité de teinte ?', 'En regardant la façade à distance, sous un éclairage uniforme, puis de près à contre-jour. Les différences d''absorption et les reprises apparaissent de biais avant d''apparaître de face.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-quality-checks', 3, 'Que vérifier aux points singuliers ?', 'Les appuis de fenêtre, les bandeaux, les corniches, les pieds de mur et les joints. L''eau doit être rejetée loin du mur, sans stagnation ni ruissellement contre l''enduit. Un appui mal réglé suffit à tacher une façade entière en quelques hivers.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-quality-checks', 4, 'Comment contrôler l''aspect de finition ?', 'Selon la finition annoncée : grattée, talochée, lissée. Chaque finition a sa texture régulière, et un contrôle s''appuie sur la comparaison entre zones, pas sur une préférence personnelle.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-quality-checks', 5, 'Comment vérifier la propreté du chantier ?', 'Par les coulures sur les menuiseries, les traces sur les vitrages et les projections sur les sols. Un ravalement se juge aussi sur ce qu''il n''a pas abîmé. Ces désordres se corrigent facilement avant réception et beaucoup plus difficilement après, quand les vitrages ont été rayés par un nettoyage inadapté.'),
  ('fr-exterior-render-paint-quality-checks', 6, 'Que faire d''un défaut constaté ?', 'Le localiser précisément, le photographier avec un repère d''échelle et le consigner dans les réserves. Une façade se contrôle par zones numérotées ou repérées par orientation, ce qui évite les descriptions vagues.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-installation', 1, 'Comment préparer le support ?', 'En dépoussiérant, en grattant les parties non adhérentes et en repérant les fissures. Un support poussiéreux empêche l''accrochage ; un support farinant doit être décapé, pas simplement brossé.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-installation', 2, 'Quand faut-il un primaire d''accrochage ?', 'Sur un support lisse, peu absorbant ou hétérogène. Sur un mur de brique neuve très absorbant, un primaire de fond suffit, parfois une humidification légère. Le choix dépend d''un test simple : pulvériser un peu d''eau et observer.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-installation', 3, 'Comment se fait le dressage ?', 'Par passes successives, jamais en une seule épaisseur. La première passe régularise, la seconde affine, la troisième lisse. Chaque passe doit être assez ferme pour ne pas couler et assez souple pour être travaillée.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-installation', 4, 'Comment traiter les angles et les tableaux ?', 'Avec un dressage spécifique : arêtes tirées au gabarit, angles vérifiés à l''équerre, tableaux dressés à la règle courte. Ces zones demandent plus de temps que la surface et décident de la perception de qualité.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-installation', 5, 'Comment gérer une fissure avant enduit ?', 'En l''ouvrant en V, en la dépoussiérant et en la traitant selon sa nature : rebouchage souple pour une fissure de retrait, traitement structurel pour une fissure active. Recouvrir sans ouvrir ne règle rien.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-installation', 6, 'Comment savoir que le mur est sec ?', 'Par mesure, avec un humidimètre, en plusieurs points et à quelques jours d''intervalle. Un enduit qui paraît sec en surface peut retenir assez d''eau pour faire cloquer une peinture.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-maintenance-repair', 1, 'Comment réparer un petit trou ?', 'En rebouchant à l''enduit de rebouchage en deux passes, la première légèrement en retrait, la seconde affleurante, puis en ponçant à la cale. Un rebouchage en une passe épaisse se rétracte et laisse un creux.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-maintenance-repair', 2, 'Comment traiter une fissure ?', 'Trois cas. Une fissure de retrait, fine et stabilisée, se rebouche souplement. Une fissure active, qui s''ouvre et se ferme avec les saisons, demande un diagnostic avant réparation.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-maintenance-repair', 3, 'Que faire d''une fissure qui revient toujours ?', 'Chercher la cause plutôt que la réparer une quatrième fois. Une fissure qui revient au même endroit signale un mouvement : un linteau qui fléchit, une jonction de matériaux, une remontée d''humidité.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-maintenance-repair', 4, 'Comment réparer une auréole ?', 'En identifiant l''origine : fuite, condensation ou remontée capillaire. Une auréole rebouchée et repeinte sans traitement revient à la première pluie. Une fois la cause traitée et le support sec, la réparation se fait par primaire isolant puis peinture.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-maintenance-repair', 5, 'Comment préparer un mur avant de repeindre ?', 'En lavant si le mur est gras, en ponçant si le film farine ou brille, en rebouchant les trous et en traitant les fissures. Le dépoussiérage final est l''étape la plus souvent bâclée.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-maintenance-repair', 6, 'Faut-il décaper l''ancienne peinture ?', 'Seulement si le film ne tient pas, s''il farine, s''il cloque ou s''il est incompatible avec la nouvelle peinture. Un film sain et adhérent se contente d''un ponçage léger et d''un primaire.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-materials', 1, 'Comment choisir le plâtre ?', 'Selon l''usage et le support : un plâtre courant pour un mur intérieur sec, un plâtre à prise lente pour les grandes surfaces, un enduit à base de plâtre pour les supports difficiles.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-materials', 2, 'Quel primaire pour quel support ?', 'Un primaire d''accrochage sur un support lisse ou peu absorbant, un primaire de fond sur un enduit neuf très absorbant, et un primaire isolant sur une zone tachée. Ces trois produits ne sont pas interchangeables.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-materials', 3, 'Quelle peinture pour une pièce de vie ?', 'Une peinture mate ou satinée, lessivable, adaptée au support. Le mat masque les défauts de planéité, le satiné résiste mieux au nettoyage. Le choix se fait donc aussi en fonction de l''état du mur.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-materials', 4, 'Quelle peinture pour une pièce humide ?', 'Une peinture adaptée aux pièces humides, lessivable et suffisamment perméable pour ne pas piéger l''humidité derrière le film. Les produits microporeux existent pour cette raison précise.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-materials', 5, 'Faut-il un enduit de lissage avant peinture ?', 'Sur un enduit plâtre neuf bien dressé, non. Sur un mur ancien irrégulier, souvent oui, au moins localement. L''enduit de lissage comble les micro-creux et uniformise l''absorption. Le lissage complet d''un mur très irrégulier coûte cher en main-d''œuvre.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-materials', 6, 'Combien de couches de peinture ?', 'Deux couches sur un support préparé et primaire, parfois une seule sur un mur de teinte identique et en bon état. La sous-couche teintée est un bon compromis pour les changements de couleur marqués.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-overview', 1, 'Pourquoi l''enduit plâtre plutôt qu''un enduit ciment ?', 'Parce que le plâtre régularise vite, durcit en séchant et accepte une finition très lisse. Un enduit ciment demande plus d''épaisseur, sèche lentement et reste plus rugueux, ce qui impose une couche de finition supplémentaire.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-overview', 2, 'Quelle épaisseur d''enduit ?', 'Quelques millimètres pour un enduit de lissage sur un support plan, davantage pour un dressage qui doit rattraper les irrégularités du gros œuvre. L''épaisseur ne se choisit pas, elle se mesure : elle découle de la planéité du mur, pas d''une préférence.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-overview', 3, 'Que change la nature du bloc ?', 'Un bloc de béton courant, un bloc de béton cellulaire et une brique creuse n''absorbent pas l''eau de la même façon. Un support très absorbant pompe l''eau de l''enduit et provoque un séchage trop rapide, donc des retraits et des fissures de surface.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-overview', 4, 'Comment se comporte la peinture sur un enduit plâtre ?', 'Elle adhère très bien, à condition que le support soit sec, dépoussiéré et homogène. Un enduit neuf est très absorbant : sans primaire, la première couche se consomme dans le support et laisse des traces de reprise.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-overview', 5, 'Quels sont les pièges les plus fréquents ?', 'Quatre, tous documentés. Peindre sur un enduit humide. Recouvrir une fissure active par un enduit de rebouchage rigide. Poser un primaire sur une surface poussiéreuse. Et choisir une peinture trop étanche dans une pièce mal ventilée.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-overview', 6, 'Quel est le rôle de la peinture, au fond ?', 'Trois rôles, dans cet ordre : protéger l''enduit, uniformiser la surface, décorer. Une peinture qui protège mal mais décore bien coûte plus cher à terme qu''une peinture neutre et durable.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-quality-checks', 1, 'Comment mesurer la planéité ?', 'À la règle de deux mètres, dans plusieurs directions, complétée par un contrôle en lumière rasante. Les tolérances dépendent de la finition annoncée : un enduit lissé n''a pas les mêmes exigences qu''un enduit gratté.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-quality-checks', 2, 'Comment tester l''adhérence ?', 'Au son, en tapant du plat de la main sur toute la surface, et au test de l''ongle sur une zone discrète. Un son creux signale un décollement ; une trace brillante sous l''ongle signale un enduit trop tendre.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-quality-checks', 3, 'Comment vérifier l''humidité ?', 'À l''humidimètre, en plusieurs points et à plusieurs hauteurs, en comparant avec un relevé antérieur. Une valeur isolée ne dit rien ; c''est l''évolution qui informe. Un mur qui reste humide après plusieurs semaines signale une cause extérieure : fuite, remontée capillaire ou ventilation insuffisante.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-quality-checks', 4, 'Que regarder sur la peinture finie ?', 'L''uniformité de teinte, l''absence de brillances locales, la netteté des raccords et la propreté des angles. Une brillance locale signale une reprise ou une différence d''absorption.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-quality-checks', 5, 'Comment juger les angles et les tableaux ?', 'En passant la main, puis en contrôlant à l''équerre et à la règle courte. Un tableau mal dressé empêche la pose propre d''un habillage, et un angle émoussé se voit de loin. C''est la partie la plus regardée et la plus souvent bâclée, parce qu''elle demande des gestes lents sur une petite surface.'),
  ('fr-interior-masonry-paint-quality-checks', 6, 'Que faire d''un défaut constaté ?', 'Le localiser, le mesurer et le photographier, puis demander une reprise avant réception définitive. Un défaut documenté se traite ; un défaut décrit de mémoire se discute. La distinction entre défaut de conformité et défaut d''usage reste essentielle, notamment sur les traces d''usure apparaissant après l''emménagement.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-installation', 1, 'Comment préparer le support ?', 'Le mur est débarrassé des saillies de mortier, des coulures et des poussières. Les joints sont vérifiés et remplis. Sur un support lisse ou peu absorbant, la surface est grenée ou traitée avec un produit d''accrochage avant la première passe.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-installation', 2, 'Comment se fait le dressage ?', 'Avec des repères posés sur le mur, qui définissent le plan fini. L''enduit de dégrossi est projeté ou appliqué à la truelle entre ces repères, puis dressé à la règle. C''est l''étape qui détermine la planéité de tout le reste.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-installation', 3, 'Quand appliquer la finition ?', 'Lorsque le dégrossi a pris suffisamment pour supporter une seconde passe sans se déformer, mais pas au point d''être sec. Cet intervalle dépend du produit et des conditions : c''est un savoir-faire, et c''est aussi ce qui distingue une finition durable d''un simple rattrapage.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-installation', 4, 'Comment traiter les angles et les tableaux ?', 'Les angles saillants reçoivent une cornière, noyée dans l''enduit et vérifiée à l''aplomb. Les angles rentrants sont dressés à l''angle droit ou selon l''ouverture prévue. Les tableaux de fenêtres et de portes sont traités avec le même soin, parce qu''ils sont vus de près et qu''ils reçoivent les chocs.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-installation', 5, 'Combien de temps faut-il attendre avant la peinture ?', 'Le délai dépend de l''épaisseur, du produit, de la ventilation et de la saison. En pratique, plusieurs semaines pour un enduit courant, davantage en période froide ou dans un logement fermé.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-installation', 6, 'Comment organise-t-on le chantier ?', 'En descendant : plafonds puis murs hauts puis murs bas, ce qui évite de salir ce qui est terminé. Les sols sont protégés, et la pièce est fermée pour éviter les courants d''air violents qui dessèchent l''enduit trop vite.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-maintenance-repair', 1, 'Que surveiller chaque année ?', 'Les points singuliers : angles saillants, abords des menuiseries, bas des murs des pièces humides, et plafonds de salle de bain. Ce sont les quatre endroits où un enduit se dégrade en premier, et les quatre qu''un contrôle rapide couvre en dix minutes.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-maintenance-repair', 2, 'Comment réparer une fissure ?', 'En l''ouvrant légèrement, en la dépoussiérant, puis en la traitant selon sa nature. Une fissure de retrait se rebouche avec un enduit de rebouchage après avoir été ouverte. Une fissure à la jonction de deux matériaux se traite avec une bande noyée et un produit souple.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-maintenance-repair', 3, 'Que faire d''un enduit qui sonne creux ?', 'Le purger jusqu''à la zone saine, puis reprendre en deux passes, avec un produit d''accrochage sur le support mis à nu. Une reprise ponctuelle bien faite est invisible après peinture ; une reprise cachée sous une couche de rebouchage se voit en quelques mois.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-maintenance-repair', 4, 'Comment lutter contre l''humidité ?', 'En traitant la cause : ventilation insuffisante, fuite, pont thermique, ou remontée capillaire. Une salle de bain sans extraction mécanique produit de la condensation sur les parois froides, et l''enduit se dégrade en surface avant que la peinture ne cloque.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-maintenance-repair', 5, 'Quand repeindre ?', 'Quand le film se fatigue : teinte irrégulière, traces de nettoyage, peinture qui farine au toucher. Dans un logement, cela correspond le plus souvent à sept à dix ans pour les pièces sèches, et à des cycles plus courts en cuisine et en salle d''eau.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-maintenance-repair', 6, 'Comment préparer avant de repeindre ?', 'En lavant, en ponçant légèrement pour accrocher, en rebouchant les trous et en traitant les fissures. Puis un primaire sur les zones reprises et sur les surfaces poreuses. C''est la préparation qui fait la durée, pas la marque de la peinture.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-materials', 1, 'Quels blocs choisir pour un mur intérieur ?', 'Trois familles suffisent à couvrir la plupart des cas. Le bloc de béton creux, économique et lourd, adapté aux murs porteurs et aux cloisons de distribution. La brique de terre cuite à alvéoles, plus légère et meilleure isolante.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-materials', 2, 'Que doit-on exiger du mortier de montage ?', 'Une résistance cohérente avec le bloc et une épaisseur de joint constante. Un mortier trop riche est plus rigide que le bloc et fissure à l''interface ; un mortier trop maigre ne remplit pas les joints et laisse passer l''air.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-materials', 3, 'Quel plâtre pour quel usage ?', 'Le plâtre courant convient aux murs intérieurs courants. Le plâtre à projeter est réservé aux grandes surfaces, où il est plus rapide et plus régulier. Le plâtre à prise retardée ou les formulations spécifiques s''emploient sur les supports difficiles, notamment quand il faut retarder la prise ou améliorer l''accrochage.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-materials', 4, 'À quoi servent les accessoires ?', 'Aux angles, aux liaisons et aux arrêts. Des cornières métalliques ou en PVC protègent les angles saillants et donnent une arête nette. Des bandes noyées dans l''enduit pontent les fissures prévisibles, notamment à la jonction entre deux matériaux différents.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-materials', 5, 'Quel primaire avant peinture ?', 'Un primaire adapté à la porosité du plâtre, alcalin et régulier. Sur un enduit neuf, il régule l''absorption pour que la peinture ne sèche pas de façon irrégulière et pour éviter les reprises visibles.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-materials', 6, 'Quelle peinture sur un enduit plâtre ?', 'Une peinture intérieure acrylique mate pour les pièces sèches, moins brillante pour les pièces humides, et une peinture spécifique pour les cuisines et salles d''eau. Le rendu mat masque les micro-irrégularités, le satiné se nettoie mieux.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-overview', 1, 'Pourquoi enduire un mur intérieur plutôt que le peindre directement ?', 'Parce qu''un mur en blocs n''est jamais plan. Les blocs présentent des tolérances dimensionnelles, les joints de mortier créent des surépaisseurs, et le mur livré brut varie de plusieurs millimètres sur un mètre.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-overview', 2, 'Que disent les normes françaises ?', 'Les règles de l''art sont fixées par les DTU, et notamment les DTU relatifs aux enduits intérieurs au plâtre et aux ouvrages de plâtrerie. Ils définissent les supports acceptés, les épaisseurs, les délais de séchage avant peinture et les tolérances de planéité à réception.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-overview', 3, 'Quel support trouve-t-on derrière l''enduit ?', 'Trois cas dominent. Le bloc de béton creux, très répandu en maison individuelle. La brique, notamment la brique de terre cuite à alvéoles, fréquente en logement collectif. Et le béton banché, qui demande un traitement particulier car il est lisse et peu absorbant.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-overview', 4, 'Pourquoi l''accrochage est-il l''étape décisive ?', 'Parce qu''un enduit qui n''adhère pas se décolle en plaques, et que le défaut n''apparaît qu''après plusieurs mois. Sur un support lisse ou poussiéreux, l''accrochage exige un traitement : piquage, grenage, application d''un produit d''accrochage, ou projection d''une barbotine avant l''enduit.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-overview', 5, 'Combien de couches, et quelle épaisseur ?', 'En pratique, deux passes : un dégrossi, qui rattrape la géométrie, puis une couche de finition plus fine, dressée à la règle et lissée. L''épaisseur totale se situe le plus souvent entre dix et vingt millimètres selon le support et la planéité initiale.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-overview', 6, 'Combien de temps avant de peindre ?', 'Le plâtre doit être sec, et sec ne veut pas dire sec au toucher. Il faut compter plusieurs semaines pour une épaisseur courante, davantage en hiver ou dans un logement non chauffé.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-quality-checks', 1, 'Comment contrôle-t-on la planéité ?', 'Avec une règle de deux mètres, posée dans toutes les directions, et une cale d''épaisseur pour mesurer le creux. Les tolérances sont fixées par les DTU et varient selon la finition annoncée : un enduit lissé et un enduit gratté n''ont pas le même niveau d''exigence.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-quality-checks', 2, 'Pourquoi la lumière rasante est-elle indispensable ?', 'Parce qu''elle révèle ce que la main ne sent pas. Une lampe tenue contre le mur, ou la lumière du jour en fin d''après-midi, fait apparaître les vagues, les reprises et les raccords de passe qui resteront visibles sous une peinture mate.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-quality-checks', 3, 'Comment repère-t-on un enduit décollé ?', 'En tapant du plat de la main ou avec le manche d''un outil, en grille, sur toute la surface. Un son mat et creux signale une zone décollée ; un son plein signale une adhérence correcte.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-quality-checks', 4, 'Comment vérifier l''humidité avant peinture ?', 'Avec un humidimètre à pointes, relevé en plusieurs points, à plusieurs profondeurs, et comparé d''une semaine sur l''autre. Un relevé isolé ne dit rien ; une tendance dit tout. L''humidité résiduelle est la cause première des cloques et des auréoles.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-quality-checks', 5, 'Quels désordres justifient un refus ?', 'Quatre, tous visibles avant peinture. Une planéité hors tolérance sur une zone étendue. Des angles saillants sans cornière. Un enduit qui sonne creux sur une surface significative.'),
  ('fr-masonry-plaster-quality-checks', 6, 'À quoi ressemble un mur correct ?', 'Un mur qui, éclairé en lumière rasante, ne présente ni vague ni bande. Des angles nets et droits, des tableaux propres, et une surface qui ne sonne pas creux quand on la tapote du plat de la main.'),
  ('in-aac-block-installation', 1, 'What has to be settled before the first block?', 'The wall''s position and thickness, the openings and their heights, the lintel bearings, and the routes for conduits and pipes. On an AAC wall the last of these is the one that gets forgotten, and cutting chases after the wall is up removes material where the wall is doing useful work.'),
  ('in-aac-block-installation', 2, 'How is the first course set out?', 'From a datum line on the floor, squared to the frame and checked for length against the drawing. Mortar is laid to a level bed, and the first block is placed and checked with a spirit level in both directions before the second is laid.'),
  ('in-aac-block-installation', 3, 'How are the courses laid?', 'Blocks are laid to a line, with the thin-joint mortar applied to the horizontal face and to the vertical joint of each unit as it is placed. The block is set down and tapped into line, not slid, because sliding breaks the bond of the adhesive.'),
  ('in-aac-block-installation', 4, 'What bond is used?', 'Running bond, with the vertical joints offset by half a block, is standard, and the offset should be consistent rather than approximate. Where a wall has to be cut around an opening, the cut blocks are placed so that the bond continues rather than being broken at the reveal.'),
  ('in-aac-block-installation', 5, 'How are openings formed?', 'Openings are set out with the lintel bearing decided first. The blocks at the reveal are cut square and laid to a line, and the lintel is bedded on a mortar that is not too strong for the block beneath it.'),
  ('in-aac-block-installation', 6, 'How are services built in?', 'Conduits and pipes are laid in the horizontal joints and in vertical chases cut before the wall is plastered, with the chase depth limited so the wall keeps its thickness. Back boxes are set to the finished plaster line, not to the block face, or the plaster will bury them.'),
  ('in-aac-block-maintenance', 1, 'What fails first?', 'Hairline cracks in the plaster, usually within the first year, at the junctions with the frame and at the corners of openings. They are shrinkage and movement cracks, they are rarely structural, and they are repaired by opening the crack slightly, filling with a flexible filler and repainting.'),
  ('in-aac-block-maintenance', 2, 'Why do cracks appear at the frame junction?', 'Because concrete and AAC move differently, and the joint has nothing to bond them. The mesh that should bridge the joint is the control; where it was omitted, the crack appears along the junction with the frame, straight and regular.'),
  ('in-aac-block-maintenance', 3, 'What causes hollow plaster?', 'A plaster that has lost its bond to the block sounds hollow when tapped and eventually detaches. The usual cause is a dusty or dry face, or a mix that was too rich and shrank away from the block.'),
  ('in-aac-block-maintenance', 4, 'How is damp at the base treated?', 'By removing the source, not by coating the wall. Splash from the ground, a raised external level, or a garden bed against the wall will keep the base wet, and no waterproof coating will survive constant wetting from behind.'),
  ('in-aac-block-maintenance', 5, 'What about damp around windows?', 'Leaking sills, blocked drainage paths and failed sealant around frames are the usual causes. Water enters at the frame, travels through the plaster and appears some distance away, which is why the visible damp is rarely where the leak is.'),
  ('in-aac-block-maintenance', 6, 'How is an AAC wall repainted?', 'Wash, fill, sand, prime and apply two coats. The important parts are the filling, which must be done with a filler that can move with the surface, and the priming, which must be complete so the topcoat draws evenly.'),
  ('in-aac-block-materials', 1, 'Which block density class should be specified?', 'Density class sets weight and strength together. A lighter class insulates better and carries less load, a heavier class takes fixings more readily and resists impact. For internal partitions a lighter class is usual; for walls that will carry cabinets or sanitaryware, or that face a corridor, a heavier class is the safer specification.'),
  ('in-aac-block-materials', 2, 'What does block tolerance do to the wall?', 'A block that is flat and square allows a thin joint. A block that is out of tolerance forces a thick bed, and a thick bed of mortar in a wall made of soft units is where cracks start.'),
  ('in-aac-block-materials', 3, 'What should the blocks look like when they arrive?', 'Sound, dry, and free of damage at the arises. AAC chips easily at a corner, and a chipped corner becomes a void in the plaster. Blocks that have been rained on and stored wet need time under cover before laying, and blocks delivered with visible damp patches should be stacked to dry, not used immediately.'),
  ('in-aac-block-materials', 4, 'Which mortar is right?', 'Thin-joint adhesive mortar, supplied dry and mixed with water to a smooth paste, is the standard for AAC. It is applied in a bed of two to three millimetres and sets hard and strong, so the wall behaves as a composite rather than a stack of units with soft joints between them.'),
  ('in-aac-block-materials', 5, 'What do the accessories actually do?', 'A galvanised or stainless wall tie connects the block wall to the frame or to an adjacent wall, and it is what keeps the panel in place when the frame moves. Chicken mesh or fibreglass mesh over the junction between AAC and concrete controls the crack that forms there because the two materials move differently.'),
  ('in-aac-block-materials', 6, 'How is plaster chosen for an AAC face?', 'A plaster for AAC should be leaner and more cohesive than a brick plaster, and it should not be applied to a face that will pull all its water away instantly. A mix in the region of one part cement to five or six parts sand, with lime or a plasticiser to aid workability, is common practice; a rich one-to-three mix is not.'),
  ('in-aac-block-overview', 1, 'What is an AAC block and where did it come from?', 'AAC is concrete with air in it. Cement, lime, sand or fly ash and a little aluminium powder are mixed, poured into moulds, left to rise as the aluminium reacts and releases hydrogen, then cut to size on wires and cured in an autoclave under steam pressure.'),
  ('in-aac-block-overview', 2, 'What does IS 2185 Part 3 actually control?', 'The standard sets density classes, compressive strength and dimensional tolerances for autoclaved cellular concrete blocks. In practice the tolerance matters as much as the strength.'),
  ('in-aac-block-overview', 3, 'Why does the block weight change the whole wall?', 'A masonry wall carries its own weight, and in a framed building that weight is transferred through the slab to the frame. Halving the unit weight of the infill reduces the load the slab has to carry and the reinforcement it needs, which is why engineers accept AAC in tall buildings where brick would add kilos to every square metre.'),
  ('in-aac-block-overview', 4, 'How much faster is block work really?', 'A block 600 long and 200 high covers a large area with one hand movement. A crew laying AAC closes a wall in a fraction of the time it takes to lay the equivalent in brick, and the thin joint means less mortar to mix and carry.'),
  ('in-aac-block-overview', 5, 'What does a thin joint demand?', 'A thin joint of two to three millimetres needs a block that is flat and a mortar that is applied evenly, usually with a notched trowel or a dispensing hopper. The joint does not accommodate variation, so the first course has to be level and the blocks have to be true.'),
  ('in-aac-block-overview', 6, 'Where does water get into an AAC wall?', 'AAC is porous in the sense that it absorbs water readily at a cut face, and it dries slowly once wet. Rain driven onto an unfinished wall, a leaking pipe chase, a wet area without a membrane, and splash at the base all put water where the material does not want it.'),
  ('in-aac-block-quality', 1, 'When should checking start?', 'At the first course. If the base is out of level, everything above it inherits the error, and no amount of plaster will make the wall look straight without a thick coat that will later crack.'),
  ('in-aac-block-quality', 2, 'How straight is straight enough?', 'The tolerance for a plastered wall is set by how much plaster can reasonably make up. A wall that wanders by ten millimetres over a storey will be plastered with a varying thickness, and the variation is where shrinkage cracks concentrate.'),
  ('in-aac-block-quality', 3, 'What does bond look like when it is right?', 'Vertical joints offset by half a block, consistently, with no continuous vertical joint anywhere in the wall. A continuous vertical joint is a plane of weakness that will crack through the plaster and, in a wall carrying load, is a structural defect.'),
  ('in-aac-block-quality', 4, 'How are ties and mesh verified?', 'Ties should be present at the specified spacing, fixed into the frame and embedded in the block work, not laid loose in the joint. Mesh at the frame junction should be wide enough to bridge, fixed to both materials, and visible at the plaster stage.'),
  ('in-aac-block-quality', 5, 'What about joint thickness?', 'Thin joints of two to three millimetres are the specification, and a joint of ten millimetres is a change of material behaviour, not a cosmetic variation. Checking a handful of joints at three heights gives a fair picture.'),
  ('in-aac-block-quality', 6, 'How is curing checked?', 'By keeping the plaster damp, not by asking whether it was done. A simple routine is to record the start time of curing, the days it was maintained and who was responsible. On a site where curing is nobody''s job, it does not happen.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-installation', 1, 'What has to happen before the render?', 'The masonry is finished, the junctions are prepared, the conduits and pipes in the wall are complete and the openings are formed. Any chase cut after rendering is a patch that will be visible for the life of the building.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-installation', 2, 'How is the render applied?', 'In two coats. The base coat is dashed or trowelled on and scored to give a key, then left to cure; the finishing coat follows and is floated or textured to the chosen finish. Total thickness in the region of fifteen to twenty millimetres on masonry.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-installation', 3, 'How is the wall cured?', 'Damped twice a day for about a week, or covered with hessian or polythene that is kept wet. The point is to keep the surface from drying out while the cement hydrates, and a sprayed facade in the morning and again in the evening achieves it.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-installation', 4, 'What happens at openings?', 'The junctions around windows and doors get mesh bedded across them before the finish coat, and the reveals are formed square and slightly sloped so water runs off rather than standing.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-installation', 5, 'When can the paint go on?', 'When the render is dry, which in Indian conditions means a minimum of three to four weeks and often longer in the monsoon. A moisture meter reading taken at several points, compared across a week, is the only reliable test.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-installation', 6, 'How is the paint applied?', 'Primer first, one coat, brushed into the corners and rolled across the field. Then two coats of the exterior product, each applied at the maker''s spread rate rather than stretched, with the second going on after the first has dried.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-maintenance', 1, 'What does a facade need every year?', 'A walk-round before the monsoon and another after it. Before: clear the drains and check the sills, the parapet, the base and every joint around openings. After: look for new cracks, new stains and any paint that has blistered or lifted.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-maintenance', 2, 'How are external cracks repaired?', 'By opening them, filling them and coating them. A V-shaped cut along the crack, dust removed, a flexible exterior filler worked in, sanded flush once cured, then primer and the topcoat brought out beyond the repair.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-maintenance', 3, 'What does algae or black mould on a facade mean?', 'That the wall stays damp on that elevation, usually the one away from the sun and the prevailing wind. North and east faces in humid climates grow biological growth on almost any coating given time, because they dry slowly.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-maintenance', 4, 'What is efflorescence and what should be done about it?', 'White salts carried through the wall by water and left on the surface when it evaporates. Brushing them off is cosmetic; the wall will bloom again until the water path is closed. The path is usually a leaking sill, a crack, a saturated parapet, or a render that is too thin to resist capillary movement.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-maintenance', 5, 'How often does an Indian facade need repainting?', 'Four to seven years in most of the country, three to four on a coast, and longer on a protected elevation. The interval is set by how the coating is behaving, not by the calendar: when it chalks, fades unevenly or has been patched, it is time.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-maintenance', 6, 'How is a facade prepared for repainting?', 'Washing to remove chalk, dirt and biological growth, scraping loose and flaking paint back to a firm edge, filling cracks and hollows, then priming the repairs and any bare render.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-materials', 1, 'Which cement should be used for external render?', 'Ordinary Portland cement is the default, and Portland pozzolana or slag cement is often the better choice externally because it produces a denser, more sulphate-resistant matrix and generates less heat.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-materials', 2, 'How is sand specified?', 'By grading, cleanliness and silt content, not by colour. A washed, graded river or manufactured sand with a range of sizes from fine to about five millimetres gives a dense render.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-materials', 3, 'What does mesh do on an external wall?', 'It bridges the places where cracks start: the junctions between masonry and concrete columns or beams, the corners of openings, and long unbroken runs of render. A strip of galvanised or fibre mesh bedded in the base coat spreads movement across a width instead of concentrating it in a line.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-materials', 4, 'Is a waterproofing admixture worth it?', 'On a facade that takes driving rain, yes. An integral waterproofing compound reduces the permeability of the render and its capillary suction, which slows the rate at which water crosses the wall.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-materials', 5, 'Which primer?', 'An exterior primer, alkali-resistant and compatible with the topcoat system. A primer on a fresh render does two jobs: it seals the suction so the topcoat does not dry patchy, and it isolates the paint from the alkalinity of the cement.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-materials', 6, 'Which exterior coating should be specified?', 'Three legitimate options. Conventional exterior emulsion in two coats, the default for most residential work. Elastomeric exterior coating, which bridges hairline cracks and suits walls that are expected to move.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-overview', 1, 'Why does an external wall need render at all?', 'Because brickwork and blockwork are not weathertight. A wall of bricks is a wall of joints, and driven rain finds them. Render gives the wall a continuous, sacrificial skin that sheds water, fills the joints and gives the paint a surface it can grip.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-overview', 2, 'What does the Indian Standard expect of the render?', 'The application standard sets out the number of coats, the thickness and the curing for cement plaster on external surfaces. In practice that means a base coat and a finishing coat, total thickness in the region of fifteen to twenty millimetres on a masonry wall, with the finish coat brought to an even surface and cured.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-overview', 3, 'Why does the mix matter more outside than inside?', 'Because external render is exposed to wetting and drying cycles, and a rich mix behaves badly under both. A mix that is too strong is dense and brittle: it does not accommodate movement in the backing, and it cracks rather than flexing.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-overview', 4, 'What does the sand do to an external render?', 'More than any other ingredient. Graded sand with a range of particle sizes packs into a dense, strong render. Sand that is all one fine size needs more cement to hold together, shrinks more and crazes.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-overview', 5, 'Where does water get into an external wall?', 'At four places, almost always. Cracks in the render, the junction between the render and a window or door frame, the top of the wall where a parapet meets it, and the base where splash and soil keep the wall wet.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-overview', 6, 'Why is curing the stage that decides the outcome?', 'Cement render gains its strength and its impermeability from the chemical reaction that continues for days after it has set. If it dries out in the first two days, the reaction stops short: the render is porous, dusty and weak, and no paint will compensate.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-quality', 1, 'How is render thickness checked?', 'With a depth gauge, a nail through a fresh coat, or a small area cut out and measured. Sampling at a handful of points per elevation, at high, middle and low level, gives a fair picture; a single measurement at eye level tells you almost nothing about the top of the wall where the work is hardest.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-quality', 2, 'How are hollow and debonded areas found?', 'By tapping across the elevation with a light hammer or a knuckle, in a grid, marking what sounds dull. A hollow note means the render has separated from the backing, and water will collect in the gap behind it.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-quality', 3, 'What does a crack survey look like?', 'Every crack is marked on an elevation drawing with its position, width and direction, then photographed with a scale. Hairline map cracking across a whole panel means the mix or the curing, not movement.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-quality', 4, 'How is the wall tested for water?', 'With a hose on a dry day, played over a window reveal and a junction for ten minutes while someone inside watches for damp. It is crude, it is unfashionable, and it finds leaks that no visual inspection will.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-quality', 5, 'How is the paint film assessed?', 'For continuity, adhesion and colour consistency. Continuity is checked at the top of the wall and in the corners, where thin coats are easy to leave. Adhesion is checked with a cross-hatch test or a firm thumbnail on a warm day, when a poorly bonded film is soft enough to lift.'),
  ('in-exterior-render-paint-quality', 6, 'What defects justify refusing the work?', 'Four, all of them visible before painting. Render that is hollow over a large area, thickness consistently below specification, mesh absent at the junctions, and cracks that were filled without being opened.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-installation', 1, 'What happens before plaster starts?', 'The brickwork is finished, the chases for conduit are cut and made good, the boxes are set to line, and the mesh is fixed over the junctions between brickwork and concrete. Any of these left until after plastering means cutting into a finished surface.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-installation', 2, 'How is the plaster applied?', 'Background first, then a level coat, then the finish. On a 12 mm single coat the mason rules off between screeds and closes the surface with a float. On two-coat work, the backing coat is scored to key the finish coat.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-installation', 3, 'How long is curing?', 'Seven days is the working figure for cement plaster, and it is the stage most often cut short. Curing is not a formality: it is what converts the mix into a hard, dense layer rather than a crumbly one.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-installation', 4, 'When does putty go on?', 'Only when the plaster is dry, not merely set. A moisture meter or a simple plastic sheet taped to the wall overnight tells you; a wall that holds damp will hold it under putty and paint as well.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-installation', 5, 'How is it sanded?', 'With a sanding block and a light, in circles, checked against a torch held flat to the wall. The point is to remove the ridges without gouging hollows, and the check is visual rather than by hand.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-installation', 6, 'How are primer and paint applied?', 'Primer by brush into the corners and by roller across the field, one coat, dry before the next stage. Then the first topcoat, thinned as the maker allows, and the second after the first has dried.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-maintenance', 1, 'What does a wall need every year?', 'A walk through with a hand torch, looking at the base of walls, around window reveals, ceilings under bathrooms and behind furniture. Those four places account for most of the damage found in a decade.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-maintenance', 2, 'How are hairline cracks treated?', 'They are opened slightly into a V, filled with a flexible filler, sanded, primed and touched in. Filling over a hairline without opening it leaves the filler sitting on the surface, and it lifts within a season.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-maintenance', 3, 'What does a white bloom on the wall mean?', 'Efflorescence: salts carried to the surface by water and left behind when it evaporates. It is a symptom, not a problem in itself. Brushing it off and repainting without finding the source guarantees its return.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-maintenance', 4, 'How is mould handled?', 'With ventilation first and chemistry second. A wall in a room with no cross flow will grow mould whatever is applied to it. Cleaning with a dilute solution, letting the wall dry fully, and then repainting with a finish that resists mould is the order.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-maintenance', 5, 'When should a wall be repainted?', 'When the film has chalked, thinned or lost adhesion, not when the colour has faded. A cloth wiped across the wall tells you: heavy white residue means the binder has broken down and the next coat will not have anything to grip.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-maintenance', 6, 'What does a repaint require beyond paint?', 'Washing, filling, sanding, spot priming and masking. The painting itself is the shortest part of the job, and the part that decides how the result looks in two years is the preparation.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-materials', 1, 'What goes into the plaster?', 'Cement, sand and water, in a ratio chosen for the surface. A 1:4 mix is stronger and denser; 1:6 is more workable and more forgiving on uneven brickwork. Ordinary Portland cement or Portland pozzolana cement both work, and the choice often follows what the site already stocks.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-materials', 2, 'Does the plaster need an admixture?', 'On interior walls, usually not. Where the wall is exposed to damp, or where the plaster is over a concrete soffit prone to condensation, a waterproofing compound or a polymer modifier helps.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-materials', 3, 'Which putty should be specified?', 'Two families dominate: white-cement-based putty and acrylic or polymer putty. White cement putty is hard, sands to a fine finish and is inexpensive. Acrylic putty is more flexible, adheres better to a range of substrates, and is less prone to hairline cracking in thin coats.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-materials', 4, 'Which primer?', 'Cement primer on fresh plaster, because it tolerates alkali and seals a mineral surface. Water-based primer on putty, on previously painted walls, and anywhere solvent smell is unwanted in an occupied flat.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-materials', 5, 'Which paint grade?', 'Emulsion in three broad tiers: economy, standard and premium washable. The difference is in the binder content and the pigment dispersion, which show up as coverage, scrub resistance and how the colour holds over years.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-materials', 6, 'What about sheen?', 'Matt hides imperfections in the plaster and is the default in Indian interiors. Silk or low sheen wipes clean more easily and shows the wall''s texture, which is a problem on a wall that was not puttied well.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-overview', 1, 'Why does plaster come before everything else?', 'Because it is the only layer that can correct a wall. Brickwork is laid to a tolerance measured in millimetres over a storey, and the plaster absorbs that variation. If the plaster is thin in one place and thick in another, the finish will still be flat, because the putty and paint follow the plaster.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-overview', 2, 'Why is putty its own layer?', 'Wall putty does two jobs that paint cannot. It fills the shallow hollows left by the trowel, and it gives a uniformly absorbent surface so the primer and topcoat dry at the same rate across the wall.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-overview', 3, 'What does primer actually do?', 'It controls suction. A dry plaster and putty surface is porous, and a water-based paint applied to it loses water into the wall faster than the binder can form a film. The result is a patchy first coat that needs extra paint to cover, and a film that can chalk early.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-overview', 4, 'Which paint belongs on an Indian interior wall?', 'Emulsion is the normal choice, and the grades differ more in binder and washability than in colour. Economy emulsions cover and are adequate in bedrooms and low-traffic areas. Premium emulsions resist scrubbing, which matters in kitchens, corridors and anywhere children reach.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-overview', 5, 'How long does the whole sequence take?', 'Allow a week for a room, and longer in the monsoon. Plaster needs to be cured and then to dry; putty needs to harden and be sanded; primer needs its drying time; each paint coat needs to be dry before the next.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-overview', 6, 'Does the weather decide when you can paint?', 'It decides more than most owners expect. High humidity slows the drying of every water-based coat, and the monsoon can double the interval between coats. Painting in the rain season without dehumidification is how a wall ends up with a soft film that marks when touched.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-quality', 1, 'How flat should a wall be?', 'Flatness is judged with a straightedge held against the wall, in both directions, and a torch behind it. The gap should be consistent across the length; a wall that touches in the middle and stands off at the ends is bowed, and no paint will disguise it.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-quality', 2, 'How are hollow patches found?', 'By tapping with a knuckle or a light hammer across the wall in a grid. A dull, hollow sound marks plaster that has debonded from the brickwork. Small hollows at the edges of a wall are common and repairable; large ones in the field mean the plaster has to come off.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-quality', 3, 'What about cracks?', 'Cracks are recorded by position, width and direction, and rechecked after a week. A hairline that stays hairline is cosmetic. A crack that widens, or that runs diagonally from the corner of a door, is telling you something about movement and should be looked at before it is filled.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-quality', 4, 'How is moisture checked?', 'With a moisture meter, at the base of the wall, around window reveals, and beside bathrooms. A reading taken in the monsoon is not comparable with one taken in April, so the number only means something alongside the season.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-quality', 5, 'How is the paint film judged?', 'For continuity, colour consistency and sheen uniformity, viewed along the wall rather than square on. Patches that flash differently under raking light show where the film thickness varies or where a spot repair was made without priming.'),
  ('in-interior-brick-paint-quality', 6, 'What is a reasonable defect list at handover?', 'A short list: a few pin holes, a mark where a ladder touched, a fine crack at a junction that is expected to move. These are touched up in a single visit. A long list of hollow patches, visible trowel ridges or patchy sheen is not a snag list, it is a stage that was not completed.'),
  ('installing-alc-panels-rc-frame', 1, 'How is the frame surveyed before panels go on?', 'The frame is surveyed before any panel is fixed, and the survey is the foundation of the facade''s flatness. Concrete frames vary, and the variation is measured against the panel system''s tolerance for packing and adjustment.'),
  ('installing-alc-panels-rc-frame', 2, 'How are the panels set out?', 'Set-out establishes the joint lines, the openings and the panel module before the first panel is lifted. The drawing determines where joints fall, and the set-out transfers that to the frame with a laser or a line.'),
  ('installing-alc-panels-rc-frame', 3, 'How are panels fixed and adjusted?', 'Panels are lifted, positioned, and fixed to the frame with the specified anchors, with shims or adjustment at the connections. The fixing sequence allows the panel to be aligned before it is finally tightened, and the final tightening is what locks the plane.'),
  ('installing-alc-panels-rc-frame', 4, 'Where do the joints get their sealant?', 'Joints are sealed after the panels are fixed, aligned and checked, and never as a running repair behind the fixing gang. The joint faces are cleaned and primed as the sealant manufacturer requires, the backer rod is inserted to the correct depth, and the sealant is applied and tooled to the specified profile.'),
  ('installing-alc-panels-rc-frame', 5, 'When is the facade coated?', 'The facade is coated after the joints are sealed and the panels are dry, and the drying is measured rather than assumed. The coating system is applied in its specified coats to its specified thickness, with the joint line detailed so that the coating bridges the sealant.'),
  ('installing-alc-panels-rc-frame', 6, 'How are openings and penetrations handled?', 'Openings and penetrations are where a facade leaks, and each one needs its own detail. Window and door openings get a flashing or a sill detail that drains water out rather than into the joint, and the sealant at the frame is a separate line from the sealant between panels.'),
  ('japanese-interior-paint-materials', 1, 'Is all putty the same?', 'No, and the differences matter at the joint and after the first heating season. Japanese joint compounds divide by their setting behaviour: ready-mixed compounds that dry, and powder compounds that set chemically.'),
  ('japanese-interior-paint-materials', 2, 'Which putty suits which joint?', 'Lightweight putty sands easily and is the usual choice for a painted wall, because the sanding standard is the visible standard. A heavier putty is harder and more durable but sands with more effort and more risk of dishing the board around the joint.'),
  ('japanese-interior-paint-materials', 3, 'What is board sealer and does it differ from primer?', 'Board sealer is a primer formulated for gypsum board paper and joint compound, and it is not interchangeable with a primer for concrete or timber. Its two jobs are to equalise absorption and to bind dust.'),
  ('japanese-interior-paint-materials', 4, 'What sheen levels exist and which should I buy?', 'Japanese emulsion comes in matt, low sheen and washable grades, and the sheen level is stated on the tin. Matt hides surface imperfections and is the standard for living rooms and bedrooms.'),
  ('japanese-interior-paint-materials', 5, 'Should I use anti-mould paint in a Japanese bathroom?', 'Anti-mould additives help, and they do not replace ventilation. A bathroom paint with a mould inhibitor resists growth on the film, which is useful in a room that stays humid. It does not stop mould growing on dirt, on sealant or on the ceiling above the shower, and it does not fix a room without mechanical ventilation.'),
  ('japanese-interior-paint-materials', 6, 'What about VOC and smell?', 'Water-based emulsion with a low VOC content is the normal interior product in Japan, and it is chosen for smell as much as for regulation. A low-odour product lets a room be occupied sooner, which matters in occupied houses and rental turnovers.'),
  ('maintaining-brick-veneer-wall', 1, 'How often should weep holes be cleared?', 'Weep holes should be cleared at least once a year, and after any work that disturbs the base of the wall. Garden beds raised against the wall, mulch, paving and soil all block the outlets, and a blocked outlet turns the cavity into a reservoir.'),
  ('maintaining-brick-veneer-wall', 2, 'What does efflorescence mean?', 'Efflorescence is salt brought to the brick face by water and left behind when it evaporates, and it is a symptom rather than a stain. A single occurrence after construction is normal.'),
  ('maintaining-brick-veneer-wall', 3, 'What do brick cracks tell you?', 'Brick cracks tell you about movement, and the pattern tells you which kind. A crack that steps through mortar joints is usually foundation movement or thermal movement that the articulation joints were meant to absorb.'),
  ('maintaining-brick-veneer-wall', 4, 'When is repointing needed?', 'Repointing is needed when mortar has eroded to the point that the joint no longer protects the brick edge. The mortar is raked out to a sound depth, the joint is cleaned and dampened, and new mortar of a compatible mix is packed and finished.'),
  ('maintaining-brick-veneer-wall', 5, 'How is plasterboard damage patched?', 'Plasterboard damage is patched by cutting back to sound board, inserting a patch backed by timber or a repair plug, taping, filling and sanding to the same level as the field. The joint treatment is what makes the patch invisible, and it has to match the level of finish of the wall.'),
  ('maintaining-brick-veneer-wall', 6, 'How are cornice cracks fixed?', 'Cornice cracks are fixed by raking out the crack, filling with a flexible filler, sanding and repainting, and by checking whether the movement is ongoing. A crack that returns after repair indicates frame movement, moisture or a cornice that was fixed with adhesive alone.'),
  ('maintaining-coated-alc-facade', 1, 'What does an annual inspection look like?', 'An annual inspection is a walk around the building with three questions: is the coating still continuous, is it still adhered, and is it still the same colour. From the ground, look for change: a patch that reads darker after rain, a joint that shows as a line, chalk on the hand when you touch a sheltered spot.'),
  ('maintaining-coated-alc-facade', 2, 'How do you read chalking?', 'Chalking is a fine powder on the surface, and it is the coating''s ultraviolet resistance being consumed. A light chalk is normal on an older coating and is removed by washing before recoating.'),
  ('maintaining-coated-alc-facade', 3, 'What does colour change tell you?', 'Colour change tells you which part of the system is failing and where the exposure is worst. Uniform fading on a sunny elevation is normal ageing of the top coat. Colour change that follows the joints is water behind the coating.'),
  ('maintaining-coated-alc-facade', 4, 'How should a coated facade be cleaned?', 'It should be cleaned with water, a mild detergent and soft brushes, at a pressure low enough not to damage the film. High-pressure washing on a facade is a common cause of damage: it lifts the coating at edges and forces water into joints.'),
  ('maintaining-coated-alc-facade', 5, 'When do joints need resealing between coats?', 'Joints need resealing when the hand test shows the sealant has lost adhesion, which can happen well before the coating is due for renewal. Because the coating bridges the joint, a failed sealant is often invisible until the coating splits.'),
  ('maintaining-coated-alc-facade', 6, 'What are the signs a leak has started?', 'Four signs: a damp patch indoors that appears after rain, a rust stain at a fixing, a blister in the coating, and a joint that stays dark longer than its neighbours. Each points to a different entry route, and each is worth acting on before the rain season rather than after.'),
  ('painting-boarded-japanese-wall-sequence', 1, 'Why does masking come before filling?', 'Masking comes first because dust and paint reach everything, and because the filling stage creates the most dust. Floors are covered, fittings are removed or wrapped, and the edges at ceilings, frames, sockets and skirting are taped.'),
  ('painting-boarded-japanese-wall-sequence', 2, 'How are joints and screw heads filled?', 'Joints are filled in passes, each wider than the last, with the tape bedded in the first pass and the edges feathered in the following ones. Screw heads are dimpled slightly, filled and then levelled, and any damage to the board face is filled in the same operation.'),
  ('painting-boarded-japanese-wall-sequence', 3, 'How flat does the sanding need to be?', 'Sanding needs to be flat enough that a straightedge and a raking light show no ridge, which is a higher standard than "smooth to the hand". The treated areas are sanded with a block, moving along the joint rather than across it, with light pressure.'),
  ('painting-boarded-japanese-wall-sequence', 4, 'How is the sealer applied?', 'Sealer is applied by roller and brush to the whole surface, including the filled areas, in one even coat. The sealer is not a paint and is not meant to cover; it is meant to penetrate and to equalise.'),
  ('painting-boarded-japanese-wall-sequence', 5, 'How do you cut in without a wavy line?', 'Cutting in is done with a brush held at a consistent angle, along a masked or a free edge, in a strip wide enough for the roller to reach without touching the edge. The line is drawn once, steadily, rather than corrected repeatedly.'),
  ('painting-boarded-japanese-wall-sequence', 6, 'How are the coats applied?', 'The first coat is applied by roller in overlapping passes, worked into the surface and laid off in one direction to avoid a visible pattern. The second coat follows after the stated interval, and it is the coat that covers.'),
  ('painting-japanese-gypsum-board-overview', 1, 'What does painting Japanese gypsum board involve?', 'Painting gypsum board in Japan means treating every joint and screw head with putty, sanding the treated areas flush, applying a sealer to equalise absorption, and then applying two or more coats of water-based emulsion.'),
  ('painting-japanese-gypsum-board-overview', 2, 'Why is paint the minority finish in Japan?', 'Paint is the minority finish because a sheet wallcovering is faster to install, hides more, and is easier to renew in a rental market. A wallcovering covers a joint that was filled but not perfectly sanded.'),
  ('painting-japanese-gypsum-board-overview', 3, 'Which rooms get paint and which get wallcovering?', 'The choice follows moisture and traffic rather than fashion. Bathrooms, kitchens, entrance halls and utility rooms are the paint rooms, because a paint film tolerates cleaning and does not lift at a seam in damp air.'),
  ('painting-japanese-gypsum-board-overview', 4, 'What is putty and why does it matter more than paint?', 'Putty, called pate in Japanese practice, is the compound used to fill joints, screw heads and any imperfection in the board before painting. Its job is to make the wall a single plane, and its quality decides whether the paint has anything to sit on.'),
  ('painting-japanese-gypsum-board-overview', 5, 'What does the sealer do?', 'The sealer equalises absorption across the paper face, the putty and the screw fills, and it binds any residual dust. Without it, the paint dries at different rates across those materials.'),
  ('painting-japanese-gypsum-board-overview', 6, 'What kind of paint is used?', 'Water-based emulsion, called jusui or emulsion paint, is the standard interior product: low odour, quick drying, and available in matt, low sheen and washable grades. The sheen grade is a practical decision rather than a decorative one.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-installation', 1, 'When can infill start after the frame is cast?', 'After the concrete has reached adequate strength and the props under the slab above have been released, not before. Building on a frame that is still carrying its own formwork loads risks loading a member that has not developed its capacity, and it risks the wall being built into a frame that then deflects.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-installation', 2, 'How is the wall set out?', 'By string line from the columns, with the wall centre line marked on the slab and checked against the drawing for thickness and openings. Setting out is where the difference between a 230 mm and a 115 mm wall is decided, and where a door that has to align with the one above it either does or does not.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-installation', 3, 'How are bricks prepared before laying?', 'Soaked, then allowed to surface dry. The soaking fills the pores so the brick does not steal the mortar''s water; the surface drying stops the brick sliding on a wet bed and producing a joint full of slurry rather than mortar.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-installation', 4, 'What is the correct bedding technique?', 'A full bed and a full frog, with mortar spread ahead of the brick and the brick pressed down and tapped into line, not slid into place. Sliding pushes mortar out of the joint and leaves voids at the ends of the brick, which is where the wall later leaks and where the plaster keys fail.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-installation', 5, 'How high can a wall be built in one day?', 'About a metre to a metre and a half of height, or less where the mortar is rich and the weather is hot. Building too fast produces a wall that settles unevenly and lifts the courses below, and it does not let the mason check plumb as the wall goes.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-installation', 6, 'How are openings formed?', 'With a lintel or a band above, bearing on the wall at each side, and with the sill or the threshold below falling outward so water leaves rather than sits. The bearing length is generous enough to carry the wall above without the ends punching in.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-maintenance', 1, 'How often should the wall be inspected?', 'Twice a year: once before the monsoon and once after. The pre-monsoon inspection is the one that prevents damage, because it finds the open joint, the cracked render and the blocked drain before the rain arrives.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-maintenance', 2, 'What should be checked before the monsoon?', 'The paint film for cracking or blistering, the sealant around windows and doors, the parapet coping and its joints, the sill slopes, and the drainage paths that carry water away from the wall.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-maintenance', 3, 'How are hairline cracks treated?', 'By opening them slightly to a V, cleaning out the dust, priming and filling with an acrylic or polymer-modified filler, then sanding flush and repainting. Filling without opening the crack leaves a film over a void that fails in one season.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-maintenance', 4, 'When does a crack need an engineer?', 'When it is wider than about five millimetres, when it crosses bricks rather than following mortar joints, when it is accompanied by a sticking door or a sloping floor, or when it grows between inspections.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-maintenance', 5, 'How is damp diagnosed?', 'By elimination, in a fixed order: is the source above, beside or below? A damp patch at the skirting is more often a failed external render or a plumbing leak than rising damp, and rising damp needs a failed damp-proof course to exist at all.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-maintenance', 6, 'How is efflorescence treated?', 'By dry brushing off the salt, then finding and stopping the water that carried it. Efflorescence is salt deposited at the surface as moisture evaporates, so removing the salt without removing the source guarantees its return.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-materials', 1, 'Which bricks are suitable for infill?', 'Fired clay bricks in the usual Indian classes, or fly ash bricks and concrete blocks where the specification allows them. What matters for infill is compressive strength, absorption and dimensional consistency, in that order.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-materials', 2, 'What about AAC and concrete blocks?', 'AAC blocks are lighter, more dimensionally accurate and better insulators, and they are increasingly common in Indian multi-storey work. They also behave differently: they absorb water readily, they need a thinner mortar and a different plaster mix, and they move more with moisture than clay brick.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-materials', 3, 'What mortar mix should be used?', 'Cement-sand mortar in the range of 1:4 to 1:6 by volume, matched to the exposure and the block type. A richer mix is not stronger in any useful sense: it is stiffer, shrinks more and cracks more, and it costs more cement.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-materials', 4, 'How is the sand specified?', 'Clean, well graded river or manufactured sand, free of silt and clay. A sand with high silt content produces a weak, friable mortar that dusts when you rub it, and it is one of the most common causes of crumbling joints on Indian sites.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-materials', 5, 'What reinforcement goes into the frame?', 'Deformed bars to the Indian standard, with the cover and lap lengths shown on the structural drawing. For an owner, the two things worth checking are bar diameter against the drawing and cover, because a bar laid directly on formwork has no cover and will corrode from the outside in.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-materials', 6, 'What plaster materials are needed?', 'Cement, sand and water for the backing coat, and a finer mix for the finishing coat. Some specifications add lime for workability, which helps on a hot site where the mix dries before it can be ruled off.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-overview', 1, 'Why is a brick infill wall not load-bearing?', 'Because the frame is designed to take the load without it. Columns and beams are sized for the floor loads, the wind and the seismic demand, and the infill is treated as a non-structural element that adds mass and stiffness but is not counted on for strength.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-overview', 2, 'What does IS 1905 require that owners should know?', 'IS 1905 sets out the design of unreinforced masonry, and the parts that reach an owner''s life are the minimum wall thickness, the mortar grade and the bond. Indian practice commonly uses 230 mm external walls and 115 mm internal partitions, with cement-sand mortar in the 1:4 to 1:6 range depending on the exposure and the block type.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-overview', 3, 'What does IS 2212 say about laying the bricks?', 'IS 2212 is the brickwork code, and its most ignored clause is about soaking. A fired clay brick will pull water out of the mortar within seconds if it is bone dry, which starves the cement of the water it needs to hydrate.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-overview', 4, 'How thick should the mortar joint be?', 'Ten millimetres, consistently. A joint that varies from six to twenty millimetres changes the bed the brick sits on, and the wall''s height wanders. It also changes the amount of mortar in the wall, which changes its strength and its shrinkage.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-overview', 5, 'How does the infill connect to the frame?', 'Through starter bars, mesh strips or toothed joints, depending on the drawing. The connection is what stops the panel from falling out under lateral load, and in seismic zones it is mandatory rather than optional.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-overview', 6, 'What are seismic bands and why do they appear in the wall?', 'Bands are reinforced concrete or timber runners cast into the wall at plinth, sill and lintel level. In Indian practice they are specified for masonry buildings in seismic zones, and they also appear in framed buildings where the infill is expected to behave as a panel.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-quality', 1, 'What is checked at frame handover?', 'The column faces, the beam soffits and the slab levels against the drawing. An infill wall can only be as straight as the frame it fits into, and a column that is out of line by twenty millimetres produces a wall that is either tapered or bowed.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-quality', 2, 'How is plumb measured?', 'With a spirit level or a plumb bob at least every two metres along the wall, on both faces, and at the ends of the run as well as the middle. A wall can be plumb at both ends and bowed in the middle, which is why the middle matters.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-quality', 3, 'How is joint thickness verified?', 'By measuring the bed joints at intervals with a rule, and by looking at the perpends for voids. Ten millimetres is the target, and a wall that varies by more than a few millimetres across a course will not sit level at the top.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-quality', 4, 'How is mortar quality judged?', 'By breaking a Joint. A mortar that crumbles between the fingers, dusts, or can be scraped out with a nail is not doing its job. A proper cement-sand mortar at a 1:6 mix breaks with a snap and leaves a rough surface.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-quality', 5, 'How is the frame-to-infill connection verified?', 'Before the plaster goes on, by walking the wall and confirming that starter bars, mesh strips or toothed joints exist where the drawing shows them, at the spacing shown. After plastering, that verification is impossible.'),
  ('rcc-frame-brick-infill-quality', 6, 'How is plaster thickness checked?', 'With a probe through a fresh coat, or by measuring at the dots and screeds before the coat is applied. Twelve millimetres is the internal target and fifteen to twenty the external, and a coat thicker than about twenty-five millimetres needs to be built up in layers rather than applied in one pass.'),
  ('recoating-alc-facade-sequence', 1, 'Why does washing come first?', 'Washing comes first because a coating bonds to the surface, not to the dirt on it. Facades collect pollution, salt and biological growth, and a coating applied over any of those bonds to the contaminant rather than the panel.'),
  ('recoating-alc-facade-sequence', 2, 'How is chalk and loose coating removed?', 'Chalk and loose coating are removed by washing and, where necessary, by light abrasion or a high-pressure rinse. The test is a hand wiped across the surface: if it comes away white, there is chalk and the preparation is incomplete.'),
  ('recoating-alc-facade-sequence', 3, 'How are cracks treated before coating?', 'Cracks are opened, filled elastically and, where they are recurring, bridged with mesh before the base coat. A hairline crack is filled with an elastic filler compatible with the system.'),
  ('recoating-alc-facade-sequence', 4, 'What happens at the joints?', 'At the joints, the sealant is inspected, and where adhesion has failed it is cut out and replaced before coating. The inspection is a hand test along the joint: a sealant that lifts at the edge under light pressure is not bonded.'),
  ('recoating-alc-facade-sequence', 5, 'How is the primer applied?', 'The primer is applied to the whole prepared surface at the specified rate, and it is allowed to dry before the base coat. Primer on ALC does two jobs: equalising absorption and improving adhesion.'),
  ('recoating-alc-facade-sequence', 6, 'How is the base coat built?', 'The base coat is applied to the specified film build, in one or more passes, and it is the layer that carries the elasticity. Film build is measured by material consumption against area, and on a large facade it is confirmed by a wet film gauge on a sample.'),
  ('repainting-japanese-interior', 1, 'When does a Japanese interior need repainting?', 'It needs repainting when the film has lost its uniformity, when cleaning leaves marks that do not come off, or when the surface has failed at the joints. Signs that are reliable: patchy sheen in daylight, a chalky residue on a cloth after wiping, ridges appearing at joints, and peeling at the junction between the wall and the ceiling or the wall and a frame.'),
  ('repainting-japanese-interior', 2, 'How is a painted wall prepared for repaint?', 'Preparation is washing, repairing, sanding and sealing, in that order, with the repairs assessed rather than painted over. The wall is washed to remove grease and dust, which matters most in kitchens.'),
  ('repainting-japanese-interior', 3, 'What about mould in a humid climate?', 'Mould is treated by removing it, correcting the moisture, and only then repainting. The surface is cleaned with an appropriate product and dried thoroughly, and the source of moisture is investigated: ventilation, a cold bridge, a leak, or a room that is closed up in the rainy season.'),
  ('repainting-japanese-interior', 4, 'How are stains treated?', 'Stains are sealed before painting, because water-soluble marks bleed through a new emulsion film. A stain from water, smoke or a marker is sealed with a stain-blocking sealer formulated for it, then painted.'),
  ('repainting-japanese-interior', 5, 'Can a room be repainted without moving out?', 'Yes, and Japanese practice is organised around it: one room at a time, with furniture moved to the centre and covered. The practical constraints are drying time, smell and access to the ceiling.'),
  ('repainting-japanese-interior', 6, 'How do you touch up without a visible patch?', 'Touch-up is done with the same product, thinned slightly, feathered at the edges, and applied in raking light so the boundary can be watched. A touch-up on paint more than a year old will usually still read as a slight difference in sheen, because the film has aged.'),
  ('repairing-alc-panel-walls', 1, 'How do you repair a crack in an ALC panel?', 'A crack is repaired by finding its cause, opening it, filling it elastically and restoring the coating over it. Hairline cracks from handling or minor movement are opened slightly, cleaned, filled with an ALC-compatible flexible filler and coated.'),
  ('repairing-alc-panel-walls', 2, 'How are chipped corners and damaged edges repaired?', 'Chipped corners and edges are repaired with a patching mortar formulated for ALC, shaped to the original profile and then coated. The patch must be lighter and more flexible than ordinary mortar, and the surface must be prepared and dampened as the product requires so that it bonds rather than sits on the panel.'),
  ('repairing-alc-panel-walls', 3, 'How is joint sealant replaced?', 'Sealant replacement is the core maintenance operation on an ALC facade, and it is done in a sequence that cannot be shortened. The old sealant is cut out, the joint is cleaned of residue and dust, the faces are primed as specified, a new backer rod is set to the correct depth, and new sealant is applied and tooled.'),
  ('repairing-alc-panel-walls', 4, 'What does anchor corrosion look like and what do you do?', 'Anchor corrosion appears as a rust stain at a fixing, often with a hairline crack radiating from it. The repair is to expose the fixing, assess it, replace it if it has lost section, treat or replace the surrounding panel material, and reinstate the coating with continuity around the new fixing.'),
  ('repairing-alc-panel-walls', 5, 'When is recoating due?', 'Recoating is due on the coating manufacturer''s cycle, and earlier where the facade shows chalking, colour change, or cracking over joints. The cycle is stated as a number of years for a given system in a given environment, and it is a maintenance trigger rather than a guarantee.'),
  ('repairing-alc-panel-walls', 6, 'What does a damp patch indoors mean?', 'A damp patch indoors means water is entering above it, and on an ALC facade the entry is almost always a joint or a penetration. The diagnosis starts outdoors above the patch: the nearest joint, the nearest window or balcony, the nearest penetration.')
) AS v(article_slug, sort_order, question, answer)
JOIN learn_articles a ON a.slug = v.article_slug
WHERE NOT EXISTS (
  SELECT 1 FROM learn_article_faqs f
  WHERE f.article_id = a.id AND f.question = v.question
);

COMMIT;
